So like a life insurance table.

227
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
And that's the objective.

228
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:42,000
Objective probability could also be looking at the odds of winning in roulette or in the game of craps or in poker.

229
00:15:42,000 --> 00:15:49,000
And in all these things, there is a statistical frequency.

230
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:52,000
Again, of course, for any actual gambling, it's assuming that the game is fair.

231
00:15:52,000 --> 00:15:53,000
Right.

232
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,000
So how do you know that this is a fair roulette wheel?

233
00:15:56,000 --> 00:16:04,000
Well, normally, of course, when people do objective probability, they just sort of assume away the problem, assume it's a fair wheel, which is fine for a homework problem.

234
00:16:04,000 --> 00:16:07,000
Again, in the real world, there's the question, well, is it actually fair?

235
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:10,000
Like, is the house actually playing honestly?

236
00:16:10,000 --> 00:16:12,000
Who makes these dice?

237
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:13,000
Who does it?

238
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
What are their incentives?

239
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:19,000
So that's the objective probability normally, there's no concern for that.

240
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:23,000
And then, of course, there is subjective probability.

241
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:29,000
Subjective probability is just a degree of confidence in the mind of a person.

242
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:32,000
So you can have a 10% probability or 73% probability.

243
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:34,000
You can have any probability you want.

244
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:36,000
It's all in your head.

245
00:16:36,000 --> 00:16:41,000
So key difference is you can have a subjective probability for something that's never happened before.

246
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:46,000
You can't have an objective probability for something that's never happened before because there's not going to be any frequencies.

247
00:16:46,000 --> 00:16:50,000
So a game, or what's going to happen in this game?

248
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:51,000
Well, what's the game?

249
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
I'm not telling you.

250
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:54,000
It's a new game.

251
00:16:54,000 --> 00:16:55,000
What are the rules?

252
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
What's it like?

253
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:58,000
All right.

254
00:16:58,000 --> 00:17:00,000
Well, you can't have an objective probability for that.

255
00:17:00,000 --> 00:17:02,000
But you can have a subjective probability.

256
00:17:02,000 --> 00:17:07,000
And I say, well, I don't know, it seems like you wouldn't go and have me play a game where it's not fun.

257
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:13,000
So, I don't know, like 50%, you might start there or you might go, no, no, no.

258
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:14,000
You're a casino.

259
00:17:14,000 --> 00:17:15,000
You're trying to take my money.

260
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,000
So odds are going to win maybe, what, 8%?

261
00:17:18,000 --> 00:17:20,000
So you can have any subjective probabilities you want.

262
00:17:20,000 --> 00:17:22,000
You can have subjective probabilities for things that never happened.

263
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:25,000
You can have subjective probabilities for unique events.

264
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
But they are all in the head.

265
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:35,000
So whenever called upon to justify a subjective probability, ultimately the answer comes down to, well, it's just how it seems to be.

266
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:38,000
And you might say, well, I've seen some evidence here.

267
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:40,000
X was subjective probability.

268
00:17:40,000 --> 00:17:43,000
There's always a question, yeah, but is the evidence relevant?

269
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:44,000
I don't know.

270
00:17:44,000 --> 00:17:45,000
It seems relevant to me.

271
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:54,000
So even if someone has been to a thousand different casinos, and you say, well, I have a subjective probability this casino will have the same kind of odds as the other casinos.

272
00:17:54,000 --> 00:17:55,000
Well, why?

273
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
You've never been to this casino before.

274
00:17:57,000 --> 00:18:02,000
How do you know this isn't a totally different casino with totally different rules?

275
00:18:02,000 --> 00:18:05,000
You see, why would it be?

276
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:06,000
All right.

277
00:18:06,000 --> 00:18:11,000
So now it's easy to go and make fun of subjective probabilities until you realize that you've got them too.

278
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:14,000
And furthermore, you cannot live your life without them.

279
00:18:14,000 --> 00:18:29,000
It is impossible to do anything without them, really, because we're always facing events for which either there is no actuarial table in the world, or we haven't seen the actuarial table, or the actuarial tables that exist might not be relevant.

280
00:18:29,000 --> 00:18:32,000
And then you have to decide for yourself.

281
00:18:32,000 --> 00:18:34,000
So, let me give you an example.

282
00:18:34,000 --> 00:18:37,000
If I go to Tijuana, what are my odds of getting kidnapped in Tijuana?

283
00:18:37,000 --> 00:18:41,000
Well, there's the rate, yeah, but am I an old person?

284
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:44,000
Do I get kidnapped at the normal rate?

285
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:47,000
Or do I get captured at the Brian Kaplan rate?

286
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:52,000
I mean, it might be the easy mark, but maybe I'm not.

287
00:18:52,000 --> 00:18:53,000
I don't know.

288
00:18:53,000 --> 00:18:55,000
And then you think about it.

289
00:18:55,000 --> 00:18:59,000
And, of course, you can ask people what they think, but then you're just eliciting their subjective probabilities.

290
00:18:59,000 --> 00:19:02,000
Then you have to wonder, well, can I trust these people?

291
00:19:02,000 --> 00:19:03,000
Maybe they're in on this.

292
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:06,000
Maybe they're in on the kidnapping, so they're going to tell you, you'll be fine.

293
00:19:06,000 --> 00:19:09,000
That's as bad as when you tell all the easy marks.

294
00:19:09,000 --> 00:19:13,000
That's when, like, as soon as I ask, you're placing a call into the kidnappers.

295
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,000
There's going to be some nerd walking over the border.

296
00:19:16,000 --> 00:19:18,000
As soon as he's there, grab him.

297
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:20,000
So that's subjective probability.

298
00:19:20,000 --> 00:19:26,000
Now, the problem with subjective probabilities is they have no necessary connection to reality.

299
00:19:26,000 --> 00:19:36,000
And that seems unsatisfactory because if you look at the world, it seems like there is, in fact, a connection between what really happens in the real world and what people think about the real world.

300
00:19:36,000 --> 00:19:48,000
It might not be a perfect connection, certainly not a perfect connection, but still, if you were to go and come up with a model of the world based upon the idea that there's no relationship between what people think and what really happens,

301
00:19:48,000 --> 00:19:52,000
you would have a very strange model of the world.

302
00:19:52,000 --> 00:19:53,000
All right.

303
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:56,000
So, now, so I say there clearly is some connection.

304
00:19:56,000 --> 00:19:59,000
You might just look and say, I don't see any connection at all.

305
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:19,000
You know, the


1
00:20:00,000 --> 00:20:02,340
Some people believe the sky is blue,

2
00:20:02,340 --> 00:20:05,000
others believe that it's orange or purple.

3
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,960
Right, and I don't know, it's just one example,

4
00:20:07,960 --> 00:20:11,880
but who's to say it really is blue?

5
00:20:11,880 --> 00:20:13,000
I mean, I remember it being blue,

6
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,360
but then maybe my memories are wrong.

7
00:20:15,360 --> 00:20:17,840
Maybe we're in the matrix.

8
00:20:17,840 --> 00:20:20,300
All of these concerns occur to people.

9
00:20:21,200 --> 00:20:25,000
Now, what can we do about this problem?

10
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,640
Well, there's a leading theoretical effort

11
00:20:27,640 --> 00:20:30,200
to go and link up these two,

12
00:20:30,200 --> 00:20:33,100
to go and say that there's going to be a connection

13
00:20:33,100 --> 00:20:35,720
between objective and subjective probabilities

14
00:20:35,720 --> 00:20:39,800
is called rational expectations, or RE for short.

15
00:20:46,040 --> 00:20:49,600
So, how many people here have heard of RE before?

16
00:20:49,600 --> 00:20:51,720
Right, so most of you, all right.

17
00:20:51,720 --> 00:20:54,520
So, rational expectations,

18
00:20:54,520 --> 00:20:56,200
actually the earliest papers by John Booth

19
00:20:56,200 --> 00:20:57,920
in the early 1960s, although he didn't call it

20
00:20:57,920 --> 00:21:00,280
rational expectations, but the idea's there.

21
00:21:00,280 --> 00:21:02,440
And then it was actually in macroeconomics

22
00:21:02,440 --> 00:21:05,920
that rational expectations first became a big deal.

23
00:21:05,920 --> 00:21:07,360
This was during a period when people

24
00:21:07,360 --> 00:21:09,040
were trying to understand inflation.

25
00:21:09,040 --> 00:21:11,600
The early proceeding models were all based

26
00:21:11,600 --> 00:21:13,320
upon adaptive expectations,

27
00:21:13,320 --> 00:21:16,200
saying that people would just very mechanically

28
00:21:16,200 --> 00:21:19,360
go and average recent inflation to form an opinion.

29
00:21:19,360 --> 00:21:20,560
And rational expectation says,

30
00:21:20,560 --> 00:21:22,120
no, that doesn't make sense.

31
00:21:22,120 --> 00:21:25,340
People are going to take all the information into account.

32
00:21:25,340 --> 00:21:28,160
And so, for example, if inflation has been rising,

33
00:21:28,160 --> 00:21:31,080
they will perhaps forecast that it will continue to rise.

34
00:21:31,080 --> 00:21:32,680
And then you shouldn't just go and take an average

35
00:21:32,680 --> 00:21:35,040
of earlier periods, you should rather extrapolate

36
00:21:35,040 --> 00:21:37,580
in the future, possibly, things like that.

37
00:21:37,580 --> 00:21:39,960
All right, so, a simple characterization

38
00:21:39,960 --> 00:21:41,720
of rational expectations is to say

39
00:21:41,720 --> 00:21:42,920
that you have rational expectations

40
00:21:42,920 --> 00:21:46,420
as long as your judgments are, first of all, unbiased.

41
00:21:48,440 --> 00:21:50,980
Which means that your mean error is zero.

42
00:21:50,980 --> 00:21:52,680
Your mean error is zero.

43
00:21:52,680 --> 00:21:55,040
Right, so, what would that be like?

44
00:21:55,040 --> 00:21:56,820
Well, for example, when you're forecasting inflation,

45
00:21:56,820 --> 00:21:59,360
sometimes you're too high, sometimes you're too low.

46
00:21:59,360 --> 00:22:00,840
And what rational expectation says

47
00:22:00,840 --> 00:22:03,160
is that your average error will be zero.

48
00:22:03,160 --> 00:22:06,160
So your high value, the times when you overestimate inflation

49
00:22:06,160 --> 00:22:08,400
will balance out the times that you underestimate inflation,

50
00:22:08,400 --> 00:22:09,880
and when you average it out,

51
00:22:09,880 --> 00:22:12,880
your forecast error will be zero.

52
00:22:12,880 --> 00:22:14,920
Right, and furthermore, rational expectations

53
00:22:14,920 --> 00:22:17,960
adds on another condition, which says

54
00:22:17,960 --> 00:22:20,200
that mistakes are uncorrelated with available information.

55
00:22:20,200 --> 00:22:25,200
So errors, uncorrelated with available information.

56
00:22:28,400 --> 00:22:31,840
Right, now, this word available is a reasonable word

57
00:22:31,840 --> 00:22:35,000
because, well, what counts as available?

58
00:22:35,000 --> 00:22:36,960
In practice, when people started doing this in 70s,

59
00:22:36,960 --> 00:22:38,280
available meant, well, was it in

60
00:22:38,280 --> 00:22:40,160
the Wall Street Journal yesterday?

61
00:22:40,160 --> 00:22:41,640
Then it's available.

62
00:22:41,640 --> 00:22:43,680
On the other hand, if it's known

63
00:22:43,680 --> 00:22:46,800
to only a few people working at Microsoft,

64
00:22:46,800 --> 00:22:47,840
I don't know, was Microsoft?

65
00:22:47,840 --> 00:22:50,240
I don't know, late 70s, was already Microsoft.

66
00:22:51,320 --> 00:22:53,440
So it's only available to people

67
00:22:53,440 --> 00:22:55,720
like on the board of directors of Microsoft,

68
00:22:55,720 --> 00:22:57,520
then that's not available.

69
00:22:57,520 --> 00:23:00,560
But, all right, so anyway, this is the simple version,

70
00:23:00,560 --> 00:23:03,360
and this is the one that is used for empirical testing.

71
00:23:04,560 --> 00:23:08,620
But there is a deeper version of rational expectations,

72
00:23:08,620 --> 00:23:11,200
which you can see in theoretical papers

73
00:23:11,200 --> 00:23:13,120
that apply rational expectations.

74
00:23:13,120 --> 00:23:15,000
So the deeper version says

75
00:23:15,000 --> 00:23:16,960
that you have rational expectations.

76
00:23:16,960 --> 00:23:19,460
If your subject of probability distribution

77
00:23:19,460 --> 00:23:21,820
matches the object of probability distribution.

78
00:23:23,760 --> 00:23:27,220
And again, in a theoretical paper, you can make this true.

79
00:23:27,220 --> 00:23:30,120
So theoretical paper, if you know what you're doing,

80
00:23:30,120 --> 00:23:32,080
and a number of people do,

81
00:23:32,080 --> 00:23:34,760
you can work it out so that you come up

82
00:23:34,760 --> 00:23:36,560
with an internally consistent system

83
00:23:36,560 --> 00:23:39,920
where there is, in fact, a distribution of outcomes,

84
00:23:39,920 --> 00:23:41,560
and people's beliefs about those outcomes

85
00:23:41,560 --> 00:23:43,880
have exactly the same distribution as the actual one,

86
00:23:43,880 --> 00:23:46,620
and everything fits together like a glove.

87
00:23:46,620 --> 00:23:49,160
Okay, so just like with Nash equilibrium,

88
00:23:49,160 --> 00:23:51,480
you may remember when, so this is when,

89
00:23:51,480 --> 00:23:55,200
if I assume that everyone else stays the same,

90
00:23:55,200 --> 00:23:56,840
I wanna keep doing what I'm doing,

91
00:23:56,840 --> 00:23:59,000
and this is true for every single person.

92
00:23:59,000 --> 00:24:02,060
Similarly, with this deep version of rational expectations,

93
00:24:02,060 --> 00:24:06,360
the idea is that given the beliefs that everybody has,

94
00:24:06,360 --> 00:24:08,400
there is an objective distribution,

95
00:24:08,400 --> 00:24:10,360
and when there is that objective distribution,

96
00:24:10,360 --> 00:24:12,760
everybody's beliefs about it match.

97
00:24:12,760 --> 00:24:16,000
So it's all the pieces fit together perfectly.

98
00:24:16,000 --> 00:24:18,760
All right, now, of course, you cannot test that empirically,

99
00:24:18,760 --> 00:24:21,600
but what you can do is test for these things,

100
00:24:21,600 --> 00:24:24,080
like unbiasedness, or see whether the errors

101
00:24:24,080 --> 00:24:27,300
are uncorrelated with available information.

102
00:24:28,280 --> 00:24:31,160
All right, now, rational expectations,

103
00:24:31,160 --> 00:24:35,120
real, inspires a very standard modeling technique,

104
00:24:35,120 --> 00:24:36,820
and any time you've ever talked to an economist

105
00:24:36,820 --> 00:24:37,660
where you've said, well, look,

106
00:24:37,660 --> 00:24:39,200
the problem is people have really bad information

107
00:24:39,200 --> 00:24:41,840
about this, and you've gotten frustrated

108
00:24:41,840 --> 00:24:44,560
because, at least I've experienced it many times,

109
00:24:44,560 --> 00:24:46,320
well, people don't know that, right?

110
00:24:46,320 --> 00:24:50,560
Because, well, if you take rational expectations for granted

111
00:24:50,560 --> 00:24:51,880
then you say, all right, well, you're saying

112
00:24:51,880 --> 00:24:53,700
that there's really bad information, right?

113
00:24:53,700 --> 00:24:56,680
Well, this is the truth here, capital T truth here,

114
00:24:56,680 --> 00:25:00,200
all right, and if people have really good information,

115
00:25:00,200 --> 00:25:02,680
then their belief distribution will be

116
00:25:02,680 --> 00:25:05,360
very tightly clustered around the truth,

117
00:25:05,360 --> 00:25:07,240
but if people have bad information,

118
00:25:07,240 --> 00:25:09,400
then their belief distribution will be

119
00:25:09,400 --> 00:25:11,440
widely distributed around the truth.

120
00:25:11,440 --> 00:25:16,440
So, this is bad info, this is good info,

121
00:25:18,720 --> 00:25:21,340
and of course, this is perfect info.

122
00:25:25,640 --> 00:25:28,480
All right, so, in the rational expectations model,

123
00:25:28,480 --> 00:25:32,060
there is endless room for degrees of knowledge,

124
00:25:32,060 --> 00:25:36,460
but it still rules out a lot of complaints about the world.

125
00:25:36,460 --> 00:25:38,440
Like, if you were to just say, well, look,

126
00:25:38,440 --> 00:25:41,760
you know, the problem with private auto safety

127
00:25:41,760 --> 00:25:43,560
is most people don't realize that they,

128
00:25:43,560 --> 00:25:45,760
that an accident could ever happen to them.

129
00:25:47,360 --> 00:25:49,000
All right, have you ever heard that from this?

130
00:25:49,000 --> 00:25:51,320
See, Jonathan Gruber's Obamacare graphic novel,

131
00:25:51,320 --> 00:25:53,720
he says, look, a lot of young people don't get insurance

132
00:25:53,720 --> 00:25:56,440
because they don't know that they could get sick.

133
00:25:56,440 --> 00:25:58,320
It was actually, like, that's almost a quote,

134
00:25:58,320 --> 00:26:00,160
and I'm like, Jonathan Gruber wrote this?

135
00:26:00,160 --> 00:26:02,920
So, you think that young people literally believe

136
00:26:02,920 --> 00:26:05,120
they can't get sick, this is a common view?

137
00:26:06,520 --> 00:26:08,400
Or maybe they actually have the rational view

138
00:26:08,400 --> 00:26:10,120
that they're unlikely to get sick.

139
00:26:10,120 --> 00:26:13,400
Another possibility why someone might not buy insurance,

140
00:26:13,400 --> 00:26:15,280
but it sounds a lot better to just say,

141
00:26:15,280 --> 00:26:18,160
look, these poor people don't have the slightest clue

142
00:26:18,160 --> 00:26:20,560
that they are mortal.

143
00:26:20,560 --> 00:26:21,640
All right, you might have heard your parents

144
00:26:21,640 --> 00:26:23,480
say this in high school, the problem with these kids

145
00:26:23,480 --> 00:26:26,080
is they don't realize they could die.

146
00:26:26,080 --> 00:26:29,740
Well, it's not literally true, I'm good.

147
00:26:29,740 --> 00:26:33,720
Anyway, so, so this is the standard way

148
00:26:33,720 --> 00:26:37,280
that economists, the economists model uncertainty,

149
00:26:37,280 --> 00:26:39,120
which is to say, look, everyone's unbiased,

150
00:26:39,120 --> 00:26:40,800
whether you got perfect information,

151
00:26:40,800 --> 00:26:43,120
good information, bad information,

152
00:26:43,120 --> 00:26:47,840
the average view in all cases is still literal truth,

153
00:26:47,840 --> 00:26:51,440
but, but, the less you know,

154
00:26:51,440 --> 00:26:53,080
the greater the variance will be.

155
00:26:53,080 --> 00:26:55,960
So, with bad information, you got this big variance,

156
00:26:55,960 --> 00:26:57,880
good information, you got a small variance,

157
00:26:57,880 --> 00:27:01,400
perfect information, you got a zero variance, and so on.

158
00:27:03,520 --> 00:27:05,880
Right, now, there are many myths about rationalizations,

159
00:27:05,880 --> 00:27:07,920
and sometimes I feel like some of my colleagues

160
00:27:07,920 --> 00:27:11,840
spread the myths, so, I'll, possibly they've stopped,

161
00:27:11,840 --> 00:27:14,800
I don't know, but, any case, it is a myth to say

162
00:27:14,800 --> 00:27:17,040
that rational expectations assumes perfect knowledge

163
00:27:17,040 --> 00:27:18,800
or assumes away ignorance.

164
00:27:18,800 --> 00:27:21,720
It doesn't, rational expectations totally allows

165
00:27:21,720 --> 00:27:25,400
for ignorance, totally allows for perfect information,

166
00:27:25,400 --> 00:27:29,080
but it does require that it look a certain way.

167
00:27:29,080 --> 00:27:32,520
So it is a theory about what extreme ignorance looks like.

168
00:27:32,520 --> 00:27:37,520
So, if you had an incredibly wide distribution of views,

169
00:27:38,280 --> 00:27:43,280
you, that would still be, you would be highly ignorant,

170
00:27:43,640 --> 00:27:45,480
you would think almost anything could happen,

171
00:27:45,480 --> 00:27:47,120
but as long as your average view were true,

172
00:27:47,120 --> 00:27:49,480
