Now, what is a misconception?

436
00:37:46,920 --> 00:37:48,240
Well, to me, a misconception is something

437
00:37:48,240 --> 00:37:50,680
that doesn't fit in that model, right?

438
00:37:50,680 --> 00:37:52,560
You know, so like a misconception about free trade

439
00:37:52,560 --> 00:37:54,960
isn't maybe free trade is better than it really is.

440
00:37:54,960 --> 00:37:56,400
Some people think free trade is better than it is.

441
00:37:56,400 --> 00:37:58,080
Other things, it's worse than it really is.

442
00:37:58,080 --> 00:38:00,880
Misconception about free trade to me

443
00:38:00,880 --> 00:38:03,640
is something like people think that imports are bad

444
00:38:03,640 --> 00:38:06,520
and exports are good, and the ideal world is one

445
00:38:06,520 --> 00:38:07,560
where you don't import anything

446
00:38:07,560 --> 00:38:09,320
and you export all your stuff.

447
00:38:09,320 --> 00:38:11,640
That to me is a misconception, right?

448
00:38:11,640 --> 00:38:13,200
Or it could be a misconception to think

449
00:38:13,200 --> 00:38:15,720
that trade must be based upon absolute advantage,

450
00:38:15,720 --> 00:38:18,960
when in fact, trade is based upon, what again?

451
00:38:18,960 --> 00:38:20,720
Comparative advantage, right?

452
00:38:20,720 --> 00:38:25,720
So these things, so, well, to me, are misconceptions,

453
00:38:26,440 --> 00:38:29,120
or, you know, misconception if someone thinks

454
00:38:29,120 --> 00:38:31,360
that price controls have no side effects.

455
00:38:32,440 --> 00:38:35,160
Right, and you're like, does anyone really think that?

456
00:38:35,160 --> 00:38:38,000
Well, I know one person who thought that until he was 17,

457
00:38:38,000 --> 00:38:40,560
which is me, because I had no idea

458
00:38:40,560 --> 00:38:43,080
there could be any negative side effect of any price control.

459
00:38:43,080 --> 00:38:45,600
Never heard it, never occurred to me independently.

460
00:38:45,600 --> 00:38:47,720
Furthermore, I never, I have no memory

461
00:38:47,720 --> 00:38:49,240
of any human being ever saying

462
00:38:49,240 --> 00:38:51,480
there could be anything, any negative effect.

463
00:38:52,560 --> 00:38:55,920
Possibly I was just ignoring everyone.

464
00:38:55,920 --> 00:38:57,560
Maybe every time I walked down the hall,

465
00:38:57,560 --> 00:38:59,720
people were saying, my God, minimum wage

466
00:38:59,720 --> 00:39:01,720
and its negative side effects.

467
00:39:01,720 --> 00:39:03,240
And I was just lost in my own world,

468
00:39:03,240 --> 00:39:04,880
not listening to these things being said,

469
00:39:04,880 --> 00:39:08,280
but it just appeared that these things were never mentioned,

470
00:39:08,280 --> 00:39:10,800
and instead that everyone around me

471
00:39:10,800 --> 00:39:14,240
had the ultra-naive view that the higher the minimum wage,

472
00:39:14,240 --> 00:39:16,800
the better for workers, and the only reason

473
00:39:16,800 --> 00:39:18,000
anyone could oppose the minimum wage

474
00:39:18,000 --> 00:39:20,000
is because you don't care about workers.

475
00:39:20,880 --> 00:39:24,880
And now to me, that would count as a misconception, right?

476
00:39:24,880 --> 00:39:28,440
A view which is not just some people

477
00:39:28,440 --> 00:39:30,000
overestimate the negative side effects,

478
00:39:30,000 --> 00:39:33,040
some underestimate them, but rather a systematic tendency

479
00:39:33,040 --> 00:39:37,380
to think that the negative side effects just don't exist.

480
00:39:38,400 --> 00:39:40,320
All right, so anyway, it seems to me

481
00:39:40,320 --> 00:39:42,480
that most intro econ classes try to correct

482
00:39:42,480 --> 00:39:45,220
students' preexisting economic misconceptions.

483
00:39:46,320 --> 00:39:49,000
Now, of course, it could be that it's the economists

484
00:39:49,000 --> 00:39:51,440
who are wrong and students know what they're talking about,

485
00:39:51,440 --> 00:39:52,640
at least on average.

486
00:39:52,640 --> 00:39:55,200
Might be, well, maybe yes.

487
00:39:55,200 --> 00:39:57,580
So there's some people who are naive protectionists,

488
00:39:57,580 --> 00:39:59,040
but there's other people who think

489
00:39:59,040 --> 00:40:23,040
the free trade will go.


1
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:02,860
and double global GDP and these two groups balance each other

2
00:40:02,860 --> 00:40:04,300
out, the average view is right.

3
00:40:04,880 --> 00:40:08,180
Could be. But that's not how we normally teach intro econ.

4
00:40:08,980 --> 00:40:12,740
And it didn't sound like what was going on to me.

5
00:40:13,140 --> 00:40:15,200
It got me interested in whether I could get some data

6
00:40:15,200 --> 00:40:18,940
to see whether my impressions might be backed

7
00:40:18,940 --> 00:40:20,140
up by something stronger.

8
00:40:21,660 --> 00:40:23,900
So I wound up finding a lot of different data sets,

9
00:40:23,940 --> 00:40:26,720
but the one that was well-structured

10
00:40:26,720 --> 00:40:29,140
for testing rationalizations about economic beliefs,

11
00:40:29,140 --> 00:40:30,960
something called the Survey of Americans

12
00:40:30,960 --> 00:40:32,240
and Economists on the Economy.

13
00:40:32,700 --> 00:40:34,200
So the survey was done in 1996.

14
00:40:34,200 --> 00:40:36,080
It's already been like 21 years out of date.

15
00:40:36,700 --> 00:40:39,280
But it would cost hundreds of thousands of dollars to redo.

16
00:40:39,880 --> 00:40:40,960
I didn't do it.

17
00:40:40,960 --> 00:40:43,060
So some other people raised all the money and they did it

18
00:40:43,060 --> 00:40:44,840
and then wrote one article about it

19
00:40:44,840 --> 00:40:46,920
and then gave me the data for free.

20
00:40:47,360 --> 00:40:51,960
And then I got like a book and like eight articles out of it.

21
00:40:51,960 --> 00:40:53,840
So they didn't have rational explanations

22
00:40:53,840 --> 00:40:55,900
about how much they could squeeze

23
00:40:55,900 --> 00:40:58,320
out of this data set, seems to me.

24
00:40:58,320 --> 00:41:00,620
But anyway, I'm really grateful they handed it over to me.

25
00:41:01,120 --> 00:41:03,920
And by the way, of course, this is actually better in every way

26
00:41:03,920 --> 00:41:05,960
for me because not only did I not have to raise the money

27
00:41:05,960 --> 00:41:08,860
or do the work, but if I had created my own data set,

28
00:41:09,220 --> 00:41:10,940
proving something that I always believed

29
00:41:11,520 --> 00:41:13,060
and that was controversial,

30
00:41:13,540 --> 00:41:14,840
how much credibility would that have?

31
00:41:16,100 --> 00:41:16,980
Less anyway.

32
00:41:17,400 --> 00:41:21,080
Far better to have some totally neutral people with no dog

33
00:41:21,080 --> 00:41:24,240
in the fight go and create the data and then give it to you.

34
00:41:24,240 --> 00:41:25,900
And then you can say, look, I didn't make this data.

35
00:41:25,900 --> 00:41:28,120
I'm just looking at it just like anybody else.

36
00:41:28,120 --> 00:41:28,900
But here's what I say.

37
00:41:29,940 --> 00:41:30,080
All right.

38
00:41:30,080 --> 00:41:32,800
Anyways, so it asks economists

39
00:41:32,800 --> 00:41:35,760
and the general public identical questions about economics.

40
00:41:36,420 --> 00:41:40,520
And notice, so if we think about economists as having good info

41
00:41:40,520 --> 00:41:43,020
about economics and non-economists having bad info,

42
00:41:43,340 --> 00:41:45,400
there still are some clear predictions

43
00:41:45,400 --> 00:41:46,500
of rational explanations.

44
00:41:46,500 --> 00:41:47,800
In particular, it predicts

45
00:41:47,800 --> 00:41:49,300
that the average belief should be the same.

46
00:41:50,420 --> 00:41:51,680
Average belief should be equal.

47
00:41:53,320 --> 00:41:53,580
All right.

48
00:41:53,580 --> 00:41:55,180
Well, then what's the point of studying economics?

49
00:41:55,500 --> 00:41:57,660
To get the variance down, right?

50
00:41:57,660 --> 00:42:00,260
Which is not a bad reason to study a subject, right?

51
00:42:00,260 --> 00:42:01,900
Low variance is valuable.

52
00:42:02,320 --> 00:42:03,780
But is that all that we get

53
00:42:03,780 --> 00:42:06,440
out of economics is just squeezing the variance

54
00:42:06,440 --> 00:42:07,100
of our beliefs?

55
00:42:07,560 --> 00:42:09,640
Or are we actually getting something better?

56
00:42:10,180 --> 00:42:16,460
Like going from thinking, you know, lie star, right?

57
00:42:16,680 --> 00:42:18,840
Thinking that that's absolute truth and that we know it.

58
00:42:18,840 --> 00:42:20,720
And then realizing, no, not that.

59
00:42:21,580 --> 00:42:22,280
You know, that.

60
00:42:22,780 --> 00:42:23,920
Something completely different.

61
00:42:24,760 --> 00:42:26,980
Right? So starting off with this view of the world

62
00:42:26,980 --> 00:42:29,100
and then ending up with a totally different distribution

63
00:42:29,100 --> 00:42:29,940
with a totally different meaning.

64
00:42:30,200 --> 00:42:32,280
Maybe that's what we're doing.

65
00:42:32,780 --> 00:42:34,800
Okay. So how would you go and test rational

66
00:42:34,800 --> 00:42:36,400
explanations using this data set?

67
00:42:37,080 --> 00:42:40,920
Well, you run regressions of the form belief is equal

68
00:42:40,920 --> 00:42:44,540
to a constant plus a dummy variable time.

69
00:42:44,540 --> 00:42:47,120
We'll plug in plus a coefficient times a dummy variable,

70
00:42:47,120 --> 00:42:49,320
which equals one if you're an economist and zero otherwise.

71
00:42:50,160 --> 00:42:52,540
Because rational explanation implies

72
00:42:52,540 --> 00:42:54,840
that expertise will not change mean beliefs.

73
00:42:54,840 --> 00:42:57,200
Mean beliefs will stay the same.

74
00:42:58,520 --> 00:42:58,840
All right.

75
00:42:59,560 --> 00:43:01,880
So and then rationalization simply predicts

76
00:43:01,880 --> 00:43:02,920
that beta will be equal to zero.

77
00:43:04,020 --> 00:43:05,660
That's the rationalization's prediction.

78
00:43:06,280 --> 00:43:10,180
Right? Now, if your beta is not zero,

79
00:43:10,180 --> 00:43:12,940
there are two different groups that you could point fingers at.

80
00:43:14,740 --> 00:43:16,600
My inclination, of course, is to point fingers

81
00:43:16,600 --> 00:43:18,240
at the people who have never studied the subject.

82
00:43:18,860 --> 00:43:20,820
And say, look at how wrong they are.

83
00:43:20,820 --> 00:43:25,180
Right? But another thing you could do is point your fingers

84
00:43:25,180 --> 00:43:27,140
at economists and say, look at how out of touch they are.

85
00:43:28,220 --> 00:43:29,660
Look at these people who have no idea

86
00:43:29,660 --> 00:43:31,720
about how free trade has destroyed our country.

87
00:43:32,860 --> 00:43:34,100
Right? And aren't they terrible?

88
00:43:34,560 --> 00:43:38,760
And they can't see what any uneducated person can see,

89
00:43:39,320 --> 00:43:41,000
which is that free trade has destroyed our country.

90
00:43:41,200 --> 00:43:44,820
So that is another possible interpretation is you could say

91
00:43:44,820 --> 00:43:46,080
we're testing for rational explanations

92
00:43:46,080 --> 00:43:49,520
of the general public or rationalizations of the experts.

93
00:43:49,520 --> 00:43:51,920
But if beta is not zero, you could definitely say, look,

94
00:43:51,920 --> 00:43:53,060
at least one group's wrong.

95
00:43:53,820 --> 00:43:56,200
At least one group is a systematic error.

96
00:43:56,460 --> 00:43:59,480
And so rational explanations fails for one group,

97
00:44:00,140 --> 00:44:01,460
either the public or the economists.

98
00:44:01,460 --> 00:44:02,860
Of course, it's possible

99
00:44:02,860 --> 00:44:04,340
that both groups are totally glued into it.

100
00:44:04,340 --> 00:44:08,000
Right? So if two groups disagree, they can't,

101
00:44:08,000 --> 00:44:10,200
now they could still both be completely wrong,

102
00:44:10,540 --> 00:44:11,740
but they can't both be right.

103
00:44:11,740 --> 00:44:13,840
Right? Basic logic.

104
00:44:13,840 --> 00:44:17,700
Right? So if one group says that the, that pi is three

105
00:44:17,700 --> 00:44:20,900
and another says that it's seven, well,

106
00:44:21,120 --> 00:44:23,200
one group, minimum one group is wrong.

107
00:44:23,740 --> 00:44:26,840
Possibly both groups are wrong, but it can't just be

108
00:44:26,840 --> 00:44:28,600
like we're all seeing a different part

109
00:44:28,600 --> 00:44:29,840
of the elephant here, man.

110
00:44:30,240 --> 00:44:33,360
Right? So it could be pi is many different things

111
00:44:33,360 --> 00:44:34,220
to many different people.

112
00:44:35,820 --> 00:44:36,160
All right.

113
00:44:37,300 --> 00:44:41,400
So, so anyway, so there's many, so now why bring this up?

114
00:44:41,720 --> 00:44:44,100
Well, this is, in principle, this is true for any regression

115
00:44:44,100 --> 00:44:46,800
where you're using expertise in order to go and test

116
00:44:46,800 --> 00:44:51,360
for rational explanations, but for many subjects, at least,

117
00:44:51,760 --> 00:44:53,360
almost no one will go and claim

118
00:44:53,360 --> 00:44:54,600
that the experts are unreliable.

119
00:44:54,600 --> 00:44:56,300
So if it's math, for example,

120
00:44:56,540 --> 00:44:58,580
and mathematicians all believe something,

121
00:44:58,780 --> 00:45:01,300
and non-mathematicians believe something different about math,

122
00:45:01,700 --> 00:45:02,980
almost no one will say, man,

123
00:45:02,980 --> 00:45:04,820
look at how out of touch these mathematicians are.

124
00:45:04,820 --> 00:45:06,440
They don't understand anything about math.

125
00:45:07,140 --> 00:45:10,760
Or physicists, or like, you know, astronomers.

126
00:45:11,500 --> 00:45:12,740
Right? So there's many areas

127
00:45:12,740 --> 00:45:14,300
where people are just not inclined

128
00:45:14,300 --> 00:45:15,220
to challenge the experts.

129
00:45:15,220 --> 00:45:18,260
But as you may have noticed, for economists, there are a lot

130
00:45:18,260 --> 00:45:22,060
of people who will challenge the so-called experts.

131
00:45:22,900 --> 00:45:26,900
Now, as long as they will not actually specify what their

132
00:45:26,900 --> 00:45:28,900
complaint about economists is,

133
00:45:29,180 --> 00:45:30,380
there's really no way of testing it.

134
00:45:31,020 --> 00:45:33,400
But if someone will actually give details

135
00:45:33,400 --> 00:45:35,380
about their story, then maybe it could be tested.

136
00:45:36,040 --> 00:45:38,480
You know, in the same way that if someone is accused of murder

137
00:45:38,480 --> 00:45:41,000
and they're on the stand, and they say, look,

138
00:45:41,120 --> 00:45:42,400
there's a pile of evidence against you.

139
00:45:42,400 --> 00:45:43,120
How do you explain it?

140
00:45:43,120 --> 00:45:46,480
And you say, look, I was framed.

141
00:45:46,480 --> 00:45:47,800
Okay? Who framed you, how and why?

142
00:45:48,620 --> 00:45:49,180
Who framed me?

143
00:45:49,180 --> 00:45:49,920
I have no idea.

144
00:45:50,520 --> 00:45:51,360
How did they frame me?

145
00:45:51,500 --> 00:45:53,040
No idea. Why?

146
00:45:53,420 --> 00:45:57,300
No idea. Well, there's no way of proving that guy wrong.

147
00:45:57,880 --> 00:45:59,760
However, you will always convict that person.

148
00:46:00,320 --> 00:46:04,420
Right? That's just too easy to just go and say,

149
00:46:04,420 --> 00:46:05,940
I was framed in unknown ways

150
00:46:05,940 --> 00:46:07,460
by unknown people for unknown reasons.

151
00:46:08,520 --> 00:46:11,040
So if you want to go and successfully defend yourself

152
00:46:11,040 --> 00:46:14,100
for the charge of murder because you were framed, what do you need

153
00:46:14,100 --> 00:46:15,920
to do in order to flesh out the story?

154
00:46:15,920 --> 00:46:18,080
You need to flesh out the story.

155
00:46:18,340 --> 00:46:19,840
Yes. Yes.

156
00:46:19,840 --> 00:46:21,020
You need to give some details.

157
00:46:21,640 --> 00:46:24,480
And the details have to be, there's got to be some way back

158
00:46:24,480 --> 00:46:24,640
in there.

159
00:46:24,640 --> 00:46:26,160
I'll say, look, who framed me?

160
00:46:26,580 --> 00:46:28,980
Well, that guy in the evidence locker over there.

161
00:46:29,200 --> 00:46:29,780
Why did he do it?

162
00:46:30,000 --> 00:46:32,020
Because he's hated me since we were three years old

163
00:46:32,020 --> 00:46:33,060
and I killed his dog.

164
00:46:33,460 --> 00:46:33,920
All right.

165
00:46:34,300 --> 00:46:35,460
And how did he do it?

166
00:46:35,640 --> 00:46:36,960
He runs the evidence locker.

167
00:46:37,500 --> 00:46:37,840
All right.

168
00:46:37,840 --> 00:46:40,100
So now at least you're starting.

169
00:46:40,100 --> 00:46:42,440
And then, especially if the person didn't reveal his

170
00:46:42,440 --> 00:46:44,660
conflict of interest, you've got reasonable doubt.

171
00:46:45,540 --> 00:46:45,700
All right.

172
00:46:46,060 --> 00:46:48,060
So similarly, when people are saying they don't trust

173
00:46:48,060 --> 00:46:50,480
economists, if they just say, look,

174
00:46:50,480 --> 00:46:52,780
they're just totally untrustworthy dogmatic fools,

175
00:46:53,420 --> 00:46:55,940
all right, well, there's no real way of showing that's wrong.

176
00:46:56,340 --> 00:46:58,500
But if they start giving details about the way

177
00:46:58,500 --> 00:47:00,340
in which we are dogmatic untrustworthy fools,

178
00:47:00,820 --> 00:47:02,160
that's where you might be able to test it.

179
00:47:02,780 --> 00:47:03,000
All right.

180
00:47:03,400 --> 00:47:07,380
So there are two main stories, which you probably heard both

181
00:47:07,380 --> 00:47:09,220
of them about why economists can't be trusted.

182
00:47:09,220 --> 00:47:13,780
So one of them is that economists are just

183
00:47:13,780 --> 00:47:14,980
self-interested, some kind

184
00:47:14,980 --> 00:47:16,420
of self-interest that's biasing them.

185
00:47:17,840 --> 00:47:20,420
So there's the classic Marxist one

186
00:47:20,420 --> 00:47:22,440
that we have been directly hired by the bourgeoisie

187
00:47:22,440 --> 00:47:23,560
for propaganda purposes.

188
00:47:24,480 --> 00:47:27,780
Right. So are you cashing in on this gravy trade yet?

189
00:47:28,780 --> 00:47:29,720
If you can, it's good.

190
00:47:29,720 --> 00:47:29,940
But.

191
00:47:29,940 --> 00:47:33,440
All right.

192
00:47:33,440 --> 00:47:37,120
So the phrase capitalist running dog or running dog

193
00:47:37,120 --> 00:47:40,340
of the bourgeoisie, this is, there's literal bribery going

194
00:47:40,340 --> 00:47:43,880
on where economists have just been actually bought to go

195
00:47:43,880 --> 00:47:45,360
and say things they don't even believe themselves.

196
00:47:46,140 --> 00:47:48,920
Right. But a more plausible version of this is just

197
00:47:48,980 --> 00:47:53,020
that if, since economists are high income,

198
00:47:53,020 --> 00:47:55,660
high job security, they're white, they're male,

199
00:47:55,660 --> 00:47:57,020
they're middle-aged, they're older,

200
00:47:57,320 --> 00:47:58,460
put all these things together.

201
00:47:58,460 --> 00:48:01,580
And if human beings in general are inclined to believe

202
00:48:01,580 --> 00:48:03,360
that whatever is good for them is good for the world,

203
00:48:03,780 --> 00:48:06,280
then maybe economists have just convinced themselves

204
00:48:06,280 --> 00:48:08,380
that say downsizing is not a big deal

205
00:48:08,640 --> 00:48:09,840
because they got 10 years

206
00:48:09,840 --> 00:48:11,080
and are in no danger of being downsized.

207
00:48:12,260 --> 00:48:13,100
So that's another story.

208
00:48:13,100 --> 00:48:14,100
Another story you might tell.

209
00:48:14,800 --> 00:48:15,100
All right.

210
00:48:15,560 --> 00:48:19,540
Another story, also prominent, is the one that says, look,

211
00:48:19,540 --> 00:48:20,900
well, of course economists think this stuff

212
00:48:20,900 --> 00:48:22,420
because there are a bunch of right-wing ideologues.

213
00:48:23,420 --> 00:48:26,240
So economics is a coven of right-wing ideology.

214
00:48:26,720 --> 00:48:28,860
If you don't believe it, you don't enter the field

215
00:48:28,860 --> 00:48:31,700
because who would want to enter a field like that

216
00:48:31,700 --> 00:48:32,760
if you've got any common sense?

217
00:48:32,760 --> 00:48:36,700
No normal person wants to be in some crummy right-wing

218
00:48:36,700 --> 00:48:38,240
ideological field.

219
00:48:38,680 --> 00:48:41,700
So of course you can get consensus, you know,

220
00:48:41,700 --> 00:48:44,680
in the same way that you can get consensus among, say,

221
00:48:44,680 --> 00:48:47,840
experts in Zoroastrianism about the truth of Zoroastrianism.

222
00:48:48,480 --> 00:48:50,160
Yeah, well, who's going to go and devote their lives

223
00:48:50,160 --> 00:48:51,380
to the subject if they think it's wrong?

224
00:48:52,160 --> 00:48:53,680
So that's another story that you can tell.

225
00:48:54,640 --> 00:48:54,880
All right.

226
00:48:55,320 --> 00:48:57,560
Now what's nice is these claims are specific enough

227
00:48:57,560 --> 00:49:00,520
that you could go and revise the regression in order

228
00:49:00,520 --> 00:49:01,120
to test for them.

229
00:49:01,120 --> 00:49:07,480
So you could, for example, add in a measure of income, right?

230
00:49:07,480 --> 00:49:13,260
And you can add in a measure of ideology, right?

231
00:49:13,260 --> 00:49:14,680
And so on, right?

232
00:49:14,680 --> 00:49:17,160
And if it's income or ideology or any

233
00:49:17,160 --> 00:49:18,560
of these other extraneous variables

234
00:49:18,560 --> 00:49:20,360
that are actually explaining the belief gap,

235
00:49:20,980 --> 00:49:23,580
then once you add them as control variables,

236
00:49:23,580 --> 00:49:24,840
then beta will be zero.

237
00:49:24,840 --> 00:49:26,380
And then you can say, all right, well,

238
00:49:26,380 --> 00:49:27,480
there's rational expectations,

239
00:49:27,480 --> 00:49:29,260
least conditional upon these other variables.

240
00:49:29,260 --> 00:49:32,840
So at least this would undermine the idea that there's some kind

241
00:49:32,840 --> 00:49:35,260
of an intellectual advantage that economists have

242
00:49:35,560 --> 00:49:37,220
where they know something that other people don't.

243
00:49:37,220 --> 00:49:37,720
All right.

244
00:49:37,720 --> 00:49:41,600
So anyway, if you go and add in these control variables

245
00:49:41,600 --> 00:49:44,180
in my work, which I think, well, I think actually I cited

246
00:49:44,180 --> 00:49:46,980
to read in a few weeks, but try to put

247
00:49:46,980 --> 00:49:49,520
in every control variable that I could find.

248
00:49:49,940 --> 00:49:52,480
So in the survey of Americans and economists on the economy,

249
00:49:52,480 --> 00:49:54,340
they got measures not only for income,

250
00:49:54,680 --> 00:49:57,720
they got past income growth, expected income growth.

251
00:49:57,720 --> 00:50:00,200
They got a measure of job security.

252
00:50:00,520 --> 00:50:02,940
They've got, of course, all your demographic variables.

253
00:50:02,940 --> 00:50:05,000
Do you think that those could slant your self-interest?

254
00:50:05,480 --> 00:50:07,660
They've got your self-rated ideology.

255
00:50:07,980 --> 00:50:09,640
They've got your political party.

256
00:50:10,020 --> 00:50:12,920
So when you put all those things in, and yes,

257
00:50:12,920 --> 00:50:15,260
the big punchline is beta doesn't go to zero.

258
00:50:15,620 --> 00:50:16,660
Not even close.

259
00:50:17,200 --> 00:50:21,500
Right? So beta actually stays almost as large after adding

260
00:50:21,500 --> 00:50:23,640
in a ton of control variables as before you put

261
00:50:23,640 --> 00:50:25,620
in the control variables, right?

262
00:50:25,620 --> 00:50:27,860
Which you might say, well, that still doesn't prove

263
00:50:27,860 --> 00:50:29,700
that economists know more than the public,

264
00:50:29,700 --> 00:50:30,720
and the public is biased.

265
00:50:31,400 --> 00:50:31,860
That's true.

266
00:50:31,860 --> 00:50:33,660
It doesn't prove it.

267
00:50:33,660 --> 00:50:38,480
But no empirical work literally proves anything, right?

268
00:50:38,480 --> 00:50:41,260
If you wanted, in the sense of mathematically proves

269
00:50:41,260 --> 00:50:44,120
with certainty, but is that, well, once you realize that,

270
00:50:44,120 --> 00:50:45,860
is that a reasonable standard for empirical work?

271
00:50:45,860 --> 00:50:47,740
Do you want to expect certainty from it?

272
00:50:49,360 --> 00:50:50,620
No. The answer is no, of course.

273
00:50:51,120 --> 00:50:52,440
So what's the point of empirical work

274
00:50:52,440 --> 00:50:55,720
if it doesn't give you absolute certainty?

275
00:50:56,020 --> 00:50:57,960
What's the point of calling up the store to see if it's open?

276
00:50:57,960 --> 00:50:59,940
If it can't be absolutely certain, it will be open.

277
00:51:00,460 --> 00:51:02,300
Yes, Bayesian updating.

278
00:51:02,300 --> 00:51:03,900
Like I've got some information here.

279
00:51:03,900 --> 00:51:05,580
I know something more than I used to.

280
00:51:06,020 --> 00:51:09,320
And for real life, that's all we need.

281
00:51:09,900 --> 00:51:11,960
Fortunately, because we don't have anything better,

282
00:51:13,560 --> 00:51:14,740
except on a math exam.

283
00:51:14,960 --> 00:51:16,620
We've got something better than that.

284
00:51:17,160 --> 00:51:18,160
Even there, how do you know

285
00:51:18,160 --> 00:51:19,640
that you really read the question correctly?

286
00:51:20,120 --> 00:51:21,860
How do you know that it wasn't a misprint?

287
00:51:21,860 --> 00:51:25,220
How do you know that that minus sign isn't actually a drop

288
00:51:25,220 --> 00:51:25,640
of ink?

289
00:51:26,500 --> 00:51:29,480
Right, so even there, not true certainty.

290
00:51:31,060 --> 00:51:33,160
Right, anyway, so someone could go and just say, well,

291
00:51:33,160 --> 00:51:33,940
it still doesn't prove anything.

292
00:51:33,940 --> 00:51:34,580
I'm not convinced.

293
00:51:35,020 --> 00:51:37,640
But again, here is the crucial analogy.

294
00:51:38,080 --> 00:51:40,700
If you go and you get on the stand and you say,

295
00:51:40,700 --> 00:51:43,260
I was framed by this guy for this purpose using

296
00:51:43,260 --> 00:51:43,940
these methods.

297
00:51:43,940 --> 00:51:46,200
And then investigators go and check out your story

298
00:51:46,200 --> 00:51:47,640
and every single part of the story is wrong.

299
00:51:48,800 --> 00:51:50,160
Should you then go back to, well,

300
00:51:50,160 --> 00:51:51,920
who knows whether the guy is guilty or not?

301
00:51:51,920 --> 00:51:54,060
What is alibi appears to be a pack of lies?

302
00:51:55,400 --> 00:51:58,140
Is that a normal inference when someone's alibi turns

303
00:51:58,140 --> 00:52:00,280
out to be completely in error?

304
00:52:01,240 --> 00:52:03,560
Now, what do you normally do when someone's alibi turns

305
00:52:03,560 --> 00:52:04,540
out to be completely wrong?

306
00:52:05,680 --> 00:52:06,840
What do you normally infer?

307
00:52:06,840 --> 00:52:09,180
If the case against them is good, that they're guilty?

308
00:52:09,180 --> 00:52:10,920
Yeah, the person is guilty and he's a liar.

309
00:52:10,920 --> 00:52:11,700
He can't trust it.

310
00:52:11,700 --> 00:52:14,080
It's like, why do you know the frame job wasn't so good

311
00:52:14,080 --> 00:52:15,420
that they covered up all signs of it

312
00:52:15,420 --> 00:52:17,180
and framed him again for perjury?

313
00:52:17,180 --> 00:52:19,860
That just sounds really hard to believe.

314
00:52:20,360 --> 00:52:21,580
It sounds fanciful.

315
00:52:21,580 --> 00:52:23,780
It sounds like what you'd say if you're totally guilty

316
00:52:23,780 --> 00:52:24,580
and you got nothing.

317
00:52:24,580 --> 00:52:26,980
So you start saying, there's a frame job

318
00:52:26,980 --> 00:52:28,540
to say I'm perjuring myself.

319
00:52:29,260 --> 00:52:32,400
So similarly, if people make extreme accusations

320
00:52:32,400 --> 00:52:35,100
about economists of being untrustworthy because they're

321
00:52:35,100 --> 00:52:37,000
biased by their self-interest and ideology

322
00:52:37,240 --> 00:52:39,860
and then you investigate it and it turns out that that isn't so,

323
00:52:40,120 --> 00:52:42,860
not only should you not go back to Gnosticism,

324
00:52:42,860 --> 00:52:45,700
you should then put the people who made the accusations

325
00:52:45,700 --> 00:52:47,220
on your list of people not to trust.

326
00:52:47,820 --> 00:52:49,700
So and then economics has vindicated it,

327
00:52:50,120 --> 00:52:51,100
even more than it was already.

328
00:52:51,100 --> 00:52:51,500
So, yeah.

329
00:52:51,940 --> 00:52:52,280
Yes?

330
00:52:52,480 --> 00:52:55,160
Yeah, just to keep going with the legal metaphor

331
00:52:55,160 --> 00:52:56,260
and going back to certainty,

332
00:52:56,260 --> 00:52:58,160
I mean the legal standard is reasonable doubt.

333
00:52:58,160 --> 00:52:58,860
Yes. No doubt.

334
00:52:59,020 --> 00:52:59,400
Right, right.

335
00:52:59,400 --> 00:52:59,940
Of course.

336
00:53:00,420 --> 00:53:02,340
Right? And the standard for empirical work,

337
00:53:02,340 --> 00:53:05,320
I guess for top journals they almost do have a reasonable

338
00:53:05,320 --> 00:53:07,340
doubt standard or no reasonable doubt standard.

339
00:53:07,860 --> 00:53:11,920
But of course that often means that then the articles are

340
00:53:11,920 --> 00:53:13,180
on topics that aren't that interesting

341
00:53:13,180 --> 00:53:17,580
because it's easiest to go and get a really solid case

342
00:53:17,580 --> 00:53:18,840
from a question that isn't that important.

343
00:53:19,980 --> 00:53:21,020
Right? You know, if you just look around

344
00:53:21,020 --> 00:53:22,780
and say what can I prove?

345
00:53:23,960 --> 00:53:26,400
Right? Well, then the odds are that the thing you can prove is

346
00:53:26,400 --> 00:53:28,340
going to be a topic that isn't that interesting.

347
00:53:28,340 --> 00:53:30,740
And I remember like when I was in grad school there was a guy

348
00:53:30,740 --> 00:53:32,100
interviewing at Princeton and he had a paper

349
00:53:32,100 --> 00:53:34,540
on estimating the demand for fresh fish.

350
00:53:35,280 --> 00:53:38,220
It's like, all right, it's a fantastic paper

351
00:53:38,220 --> 00:53:41,680
about something of no interest to anyone except maybe people

352
00:53:41,680 --> 00:53:43,140
in the fishing industry.

353
00:53:43,660 --> 00:53:45,160
But we're not in the fishing industry.

354
00:53:45,160 --> 00:53:48,060
So, but you know, like the econometrics were very fancy

355
00:53:48,060 --> 00:53:48,860
from what I understand.

356
00:53:48,860 --> 00:53:50,560
But didn't move me.

357
00:53:50,560 --> 00:53:55,260
But of course as you go down the pecking order of journals,

358
00:53:55,260 --> 00:53:57,360
then the odds of being able to publish something

359
00:53:57,360 --> 00:54:00,300
where the evidence is less convincing

360
00:54:00,640 --> 00:54:01,940
by the topics more important is higher.

361
00:54:02,780 --> 00:54:04,780
The other way, of course, you get articles

362
00:54:04,780 --> 00:54:07,320
on bigger questions in top journals is

363
00:54:07,320 --> 00:54:08,260
if you're a Nobel Prize winner.

364
00:54:08,260 --> 00:54:10,540
And then you get to go and write your opinions and say, hey,

365
00:54:10,540 --> 00:54:11,820
I got a Nobel Prize.

366
00:54:11,820 --> 00:54:14,420
So the way things seem to me is interesting.

367
00:54:15,040 --> 00:54:17,520
But the way things seem to Brian, not so interesting.

368
00:54:17,520 --> 00:54:18,700
Not lacking the Nobel Prize.

369
00:54:19,260 --> 00:54:20,240
All right.

370
00:54:21,520 --> 00:54:22,660
So questions.

371
00:54:22,920 --> 00:54:25,500
Questions about testing economic beliefs

372
00:54:25,500 --> 00:54:27,040
about economics for rational expectations.

373
00:54:27,300 --> 00:54:28,600
Two questions.

374
00:54:28,720 --> 00:54:28,960
Sure.

375
00:54:29,100 --> 00:54:33,620
One in reference to this truth distribution.

376
00:54:33,740 --> 00:54:36,080
What does it mean to be positively

377
00:54:36,080 --> 00:54:38,860
versus negatively awake in truth?

378
00:54:38,860 --> 00:54:41,180
Well, so like the most obvious ones are things

379
00:54:41,180 --> 00:54:43,300
like how much money am I going to make from the sale

380
00:54:43,300 --> 00:54:43,740
of this book?

381
00:54:44,180 --> 00:54:45,340
A positive error would be thinking

382
00:54:45,340 --> 00:54:46,480
that you make more money than you're going

383
00:54:46,480 --> 00:54:47,560
to make more money than you actually make.

384
00:54:47,560 --> 00:54:48,940
And negative errors if you make less money.

385
00:54:48,940 --> 00:54:51,960
So, you know, sort of like for any economic variable,

386
00:54:51,960 --> 00:54:52,820
you can think of it like that.

387
00:54:52,820 --> 00:54:55,200
So you can think that the trade deficit will be higher

388
00:54:55,200 --> 00:54:56,900
than it really is or lower than it really is.

389
00:54:57,260 --> 00:54:58,560
You can think that inflation will be higher

390
00:54:58,560 --> 00:55:00,640
than it really is or lower than it really is.

391
00:55:01,340 --> 00:55:05,000
So it means, you know, so anything in that ballpark.

392
00:55:05,000 --> 00:55:06,180
And of course, you know, could be,

393
00:55:06,460 --> 00:55:08,020
personally be taller than you expect or shorter

394
00:55:08,020 --> 00:55:10,420
than you expect as in, well, I thought you would be taller

395
00:55:10,540 --> 00:55:11,540
when you meet a famous person.

396
00:55:12,240 --> 00:55:12,540
Yeah, so.

397
00:55:12,820 --> 00:55:14,780
So I always expect famous people to be tall.

398
00:55:15,440 --> 00:55:17,060
Like the least average or taller.

399
00:55:17,580 --> 00:55:20,300
Right? Unless it's like they're known to be like Danny DeVito

400
00:55:20,300 --> 00:55:21,080
or something like that.

401
00:55:21,080 --> 00:55:23,120
But so anyway, that would be a case

402
00:55:23,120 --> 00:55:24,780
of not having rational expectations.

403
00:55:24,780 --> 00:55:27,540
Because of course, tons of famous people who are short.

404
00:55:27,540 --> 00:55:29,360
I mean, there is at least a story

405
00:55:29,360 --> 00:55:30,900
about why we think they're so tall.

406
00:55:30,900 --> 00:55:33,420
It's like why do we think Tom Cruise is way taller

407
00:55:33,420 --> 00:55:34,120
than he really is?

408
00:55:34,120 --> 00:55:34,260
The camera.

409
00:55:34,260 --> 00:55:34,440
Yes.

410
00:55:34,440 --> 00:55:38,220
It's hard for us to think of a really short man

411
00:55:38,700 --> 00:55:42,020
as being an action hero.

412
00:55:42,360 --> 00:55:45,140
So we just change the camera around and make him look

413
00:55:45,140 --> 00:55:46,860
at least as tall as everyone that he's around.

414
00:55:47,520 --> 00:55:51,060
So you give him special shoes or you just move, like you just do,

415
00:55:51,060 --> 00:55:53,320
like you know, like Hobbit forced perspective shot.

416
00:55:53,320 --> 00:55:55,280
There's lots of ways that you can handle that.

417
00:55:55,720 --> 00:55:56,900
Yeah, oh sure.

418
00:55:56,900 --> 00:55:59,060
So the belief axis, this is belief

419
00:55:59,060 --> 00:56:00,820
in a particular economic proposition.

420
00:56:00,880 --> 00:56:03,820
So the answer is there's 37 different questions

421
00:56:03,820 --> 00:56:04,420
that I look at.

422
00:56:04,960 --> 00:56:08,200
So a lot of them are in the form is, you know,

423
00:56:08,200 --> 00:56:10,400
is X a major property?

424
00:56:10,400 --> 00:56:12,540
So why the economy isn't doing as well as it otherwise would?

425
00:56:12,540 --> 00:56:14,000
A minor reason or not a reason?

426
00:56:14,700 --> 00:56:17,340
So it's one where you can easily rank order them

427
00:56:17,340 --> 00:56:18,640
as like zero, one, or two.

428
00:56:18,640 --> 00:56:21,380
Then there's some where the thing is the following, good,

429
00:56:21,380 --> 00:56:22,780
neutral, or bad for the economy.

430
00:56:23,340 --> 00:56:26,780
And so, you know, then there's a number

431
00:56:26,780 --> 00:56:28,020
of other variations on that.

432
00:56:28,020 --> 00:56:29,920
So sometimes beta is positive, sometimes it's negative.

433
00:56:29,920 --> 00:56:30,180
Yeah, yeah.

434
00:56:30,460 --> 00:56:32,320
But the economists tend to be clumped together

435
00:56:32,320 --> 00:56:33,360
and the general public.

436
00:56:33,360 --> 00:56:35,420
Yeah, so basically this is testing whether the,

437
00:56:35,420 --> 00:56:37,520
so like is beta actually equal to zero?

438
00:56:37,520 --> 00:56:40,120
So you, you know, like essentially you could just flip

439
00:56:40,120 --> 00:56:42,400
the coding of the question and make it positive or negative.

440
00:56:42,860 --> 00:56:46,740
And then is the public kind of all over the place

441
00:56:46,740 --> 00:56:47,940
and the economists clumped together?

442
00:56:48,240 --> 00:56:49,460
Or are the public.

443
00:56:49,820 --> 00:56:51,060
No, no, no, no, no.

444
00:56:51,060 --> 00:56:53,320
So the public tends to be very clumped together.

445
00:56:53,700 --> 00:56:55,340
On most issues, not all.

446
00:56:55,660 --> 00:56:57,980
But especially for like anything involving international trade,

447
00:56:57,980 --> 00:56:59,580
the public just tends to clump together

448
00:56:59,580 --> 00:57:01,340
in extreme paranoia and negativity.

449
00:57:01,340 --> 00:57:03,040
And actually on those, economists tend

450
00:57:03,040 --> 00:57:05,240
to clump together at high positivity.

451
00:57:06,240 --> 00:57:08,960
So, you know, like some of the biggest questions, things like,

452
00:57:09,320 --> 00:57:13,280
is excessive foreign aid a major reason, a minor reason,

453
00:57:13,280 --> 00:57:15,620
or not a reason why the American economy is not doing better

454
00:57:15,620 --> 00:57:16,500
than it otherwise would?

455
00:57:16,840 --> 00:57:18,880
And almost all, almost all economists say it's not a

456
00:57:18,880 --> 00:57:21,280
reason, and almost all non-economists say it's a

457
00:57:21,280 --> 00:57:21,880
major reason.

458
00:57:22,520 --> 00:57:23,320
So things like that.

459
00:57:23,620 --> 00:57:25,220
So you see big differences of that sort.

460
00:57:25,840 --> 00:57:28,160
Yeah, so it does, it does vary.

461
00:57:28,160 --> 00:57:30,400
But say that you actually, non-economists tend

462
00:57:30,400 --> 00:57:31,960
to cluster more.

463
00:57:32,360 --> 00:57:34,240
And how do they cluster?

464
00:57:34,500 --> 00:57:38,040
Basically, almost exactly as my stereotypes of what I thought

465
00:57:38,040 --> 00:57:40,340
when I was 17 before I studied economics, would say.

466
00:57:40,960 --> 00:57:44,000
So, you know, like scapegoating foreigners, extreme xenophobia.

467
00:57:44,520 --> 00:57:47,400
Like on every, any possible issue where foreigners rise,

468
00:57:47,400 --> 00:57:51,200
then people are very nervous that foreigners are doing us harm.

469
00:57:51,640 --> 00:57:53,260
And then, just like a general hostility

470
00:57:53,260 --> 00:57:55,620
to markets, also very strong.

471
00:57:56,000 --> 00:57:58,280
Hostility to anything that might ever cost a loose,

472
00:57:58,280 --> 00:57:59,480
got to cost someone to lose a job.

473
00:57:59,480 --> 00:58:00,100
Very strong.

474
00:58:00,100 --> 00:58:00,480
All right.

475
00:58:01,400 --> 00:58:03,680
That's one where, even a lot of economists will say,

476
00:58:03,680 --> 00:58:05,180
it's not really the public disagrees with us.

477
00:58:05,180 --> 00:58:06,480
It's that we take a long-run view,

478
00:58:06,480 --> 00:58:07,940
and the public takes a short-run view.

479
00:58:08,540 --> 00:58:12,020
But, there was one question that actually asked about the effects

480
00:58:12,020 --> 00:58:15,600
of, of things like downsizing 20 years in the future.

481
00:58:16,220 --> 00:58:18,680
And that question actually is an unusually large gap

482
00:58:18,680 --> 00:58:19,800
between economists and the public.

483
00:58:20,260 --> 00:58:21,680
So just saying there's a difference

484
00:58:21,680 --> 00:58:23,300
in time horizon doesn't seem to be right.

485
00:58:23,300 --> 00:58:25,840
Yeah, yeah, Nathan?

486
00:58:25,840 --> 00:58:31,680
So, it seems like most ideology measures map a left

487
00:58:31,680 --> 00:58:32,160
to right thing.

488
00:58:32,160 --> 00:58:32,360
Yes.

489
00:58:32,360 --> 00:58:32,960
Which works for.

490
00:58:32,960 --> 00:58:33,300
That is correct.

491
00:58:33,300 --> 00:58:34,700
Economists are right-wing ideologues.

492
00:58:34,780 --> 00:58:38,360
But, what if someone were to make some counter-argument

493
00:58:38,360 --> 00:58:41,720
that the very differences that you proposed are ideological.

494
00:58:41,720 --> 00:58:43,140
So, it's not that we're right-wing ideologues.

495
00:58:43,140 --> 00:58:45,320
It's that we're globalist ideologues.

496
00:58:45,320 --> 00:58:45,680
Right, right.

497
00:58:45,680 --> 00:58:46,020
Something like that.

498
00:58:46,020 --> 00:58:46,740
Yeah, fair question.

499
00:58:46,740 --> 00:58:49,740
So, this ideology measure also had an option

500
00:58:49,740 --> 00:58:51,280
of I don't think in left-right terms.

501
00:58:51,540 --> 00:58:51,940
Ah.

502
00:58:52,220 --> 00:58:55,620
So, that, you know, that is not, that is not decisive

503
00:58:55,620 --> 00:58:57,400
because there's a lot of different variations on that.

504
00:58:57,860 --> 00:58:59,020
And of course, you could also say, well,

505
00:58:59,020 --> 00:59:00,180
of course economists don't admit

506
00:59:00,180 --> 00:59:01,620
that they have a different ideology.

507
00:59:01,820 --> 00:59:03,460
They pretend like they're just normal liberals

508
00:59:03,460 --> 00:59:04,620
and conservatives, but they're not.

509
00:59:05,720 --> 00:59:08,120
So, again, the main thing I would say is

510
00:59:08,120 --> 00:59:10,720
that the accusers generally say that it is right-wing,

511
00:59:10,900 --> 00:59:12,060
right-wing ideology.

512
00:59:12,640 --> 00:59:16,140
So, that, you know, so there is another claim about being

513
00:59:16,140 --> 00:59:18,380
about being global, globalist or cosmopolitan ideology.

514
00:59:18,380 --> 00:59:20,620
That's actually a much narrower range of the critics.

515
00:59:21,060 --> 00:59:23,380
It's mostly just saying it's right-wing.

516
00:59:23,380 --> 00:59:27,420
I mean, so, that one actually, if you know basic facts,

517
00:59:27,420 --> 00:59:28,640
you know it can't possibly work

518
00:59:28,640 --> 00:59:31,900
because the typical economist is a moderate Democrat

519
00:59:31,900 --> 00:59:33,460
and is not right-wing in any sense.

520
00:59:34,260 --> 00:59:36,360
So, other than the fact

521
00:59:36,360 --> 00:59:38,760
that they will occasionally endorse a very right-wing view

522
00:59:39,600 --> 00:59:40,040
on something.

523
00:59:40,700 --> 00:59:42,060
But, again, econometrically,

524
00:59:42,340 --> 00:59:44,580
if someone says they're a liberal Democrat,

525
00:59:44,580 --> 00:59:46,740
but they think downsizing is good,

526
00:59:48,620 --> 00:59:51,020
which is actually a very common thing for liberal Democrat,

527
00:59:51,020 --> 00:59:52,760
liberal Democratic economists to say.

528
00:59:52,760 --> 00:59:56,100
If economists were liberals and Democrats,

529
00:59:56,500 --> 00:59:58,260
say something that is very different

530
00:59:58,260 --> 01:00:24,720
from what other liberals and Democrats say.


1
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:05,720
And what does that do to the value of the econ coefficient?

2
01:00:05,720 --> 01:00:07,120
What does it do to it?

3
01:00:07,120 --> 01:00:08,120
It makes it big.

4
01:00:08,120 --> 01:00:09,680
Because the whole idea of regression

5
01:00:09,680 --> 01:00:12,440
is that you are trying to compare people

6
01:00:12,440 --> 01:00:15,760
who are alike in every other way, but different in one thing.

7
01:00:15,760 --> 01:00:18,760
So if economists tend to be in the group that's especially

8
01:00:18,760 --> 01:00:22,440
worried about downsizing, except because they're economists,

9
01:00:22,440 --> 01:00:23,960
and only because they're economists,

10
01:00:23,960 --> 01:00:25,720
they have a very different view, then

11
01:00:25,720 --> 01:00:31,880
that actually amplifies the effect of economic training.

12
01:00:31,880 --> 01:00:32,960
So other questions?

13
01:00:32,960 --> 01:00:33,760
Yeah, Ash?

14
01:00:33,760 --> 01:00:35,840
Just on that notion of subjective

15
01:00:35,840 --> 01:00:38,240
versus objective probability, do you know,

16
01:00:38,240 --> 01:00:39,720
this is a weird question, but do you

17
01:00:39,720 --> 01:00:41,320
know if there's any literature that

18
01:00:41,320 --> 01:00:45,080
relates objective probability to cardinal probability

19
01:00:45,080 --> 01:00:47,520
and subjective probability to ordinal probability?

20
01:00:47,520 --> 01:00:49,800
So I don't know anything about ordinal

21
01:00:49,800 --> 01:00:50,960
versus cardinal probability.

22
01:00:50,960 --> 01:00:53,200
It's a very small topic.

23
01:00:53,200 --> 01:00:57,960
Sounds like it is a topic, but it's not a big topic.

24
01:00:57,960 --> 01:01:01,240
So again, what an ordinal probability would be like?

25
01:01:01,240 --> 01:01:03,200
If you say, well, I can say that one probability is

26
01:01:03,200 --> 01:01:05,200
more likely than another, but I can't tell you

27
01:01:05,200 --> 01:01:07,000
how much more likely?

28
01:01:07,000 --> 01:01:09,520
All right, so I guess you can imagine a,

29
01:01:09,520 --> 01:01:11,600
I'm sure a mathematician could design that system,

30
01:01:11,600 --> 01:01:15,440
but it would be hard to explain, at least to me.

31
01:01:15,440 --> 01:01:17,040
Certainly hard for me to explain.

32
01:01:17,040 --> 01:01:18,800
It would also be hard to explain it to me,

33
01:01:18,800 --> 01:01:20,480
because I would sit there saying, what?

34
01:01:20,480 --> 01:01:22,360
No!

35
01:01:22,360 --> 01:01:25,360
What?

36
01:01:25,360 --> 01:01:28,280
All right, so moving on then.

37
01:01:28,280 --> 01:01:30,840
All right, now, we are back in week two.

38
01:01:30,840 --> 01:01:34,320
We already did a very general model of economic action

39
01:01:34,320 --> 01:01:37,720
under uncertainty, which is the Erdo-de-Bruin model.

40
01:01:37,720 --> 01:01:40,800
You can remember the complete contingent claims market

41
01:01:40,800 --> 01:01:43,760
model, so that is a model of choice under uncertainty.

42
01:01:43,760 --> 01:01:45,760
But complete contingent claims markets

43
01:01:45,760 --> 01:01:48,040
is a highly unrealistic assumption.

44
01:01:48,040 --> 01:01:50,880
There are most of the time, you can't go and say, look,

45
01:01:50,880 --> 01:01:53,360
I want to go and get a computer, but I only

46
01:01:53,360 --> 01:01:57,720
want it if the processing speed of computer chips

47
01:01:57,720 --> 01:01:59,640
grows at a slower rate than this.

48
01:01:59,640 --> 01:02:02,200
How much will you charge me for that computer?

49
01:02:02,200 --> 01:02:04,400
And normally, you'll say, we just won't.

50
01:02:04,400 --> 01:02:06,720
No, leave our store.

51
01:02:06,720 --> 01:02:09,000
We don't want to go and start making weird contracts

52
01:02:09,000 --> 01:02:10,960
with every crackpot who comes off the street

53
01:02:10,960 --> 01:02:12,160
and want something different.

54
01:02:12,160 --> 01:02:14,840
Say, look, we got three options here.

55
01:02:14,840 --> 01:02:18,320
We got this computer, that computer, and the other.

56
01:02:18,320 --> 01:02:21,640
But I want 2 thirds of the first one and 1 third of the second

57
01:02:21,640 --> 01:02:22,520
one.

58
01:02:22,520 --> 01:02:24,680
We can't say no.

59
01:02:24,680 --> 01:02:27,800
All right, so is there any other approach

60
01:02:27,800 --> 01:02:29,960
to economic action under uncertainty?

61
01:02:29,960 --> 01:02:32,320
And the answer, of course, is yes.

62
01:02:32,320 --> 01:02:34,960
There's a very general theory of economic action

63
01:02:34,960 --> 01:02:53,760
under uncertainty, and its name is associated.

64
01:02:53,760 --> 01:02:55,480
Partly, I teach this because I've

65
01:02:55,480 --> 01:02:58,600
had a horrible experience at some economic conferences,

66
01:02:58,600 --> 01:03:00,800
and I want to make sure that none of you

67
01:03:00,800 --> 01:03:02,000
are ever part of the problem.

68
01:03:02,000 --> 01:03:03,960
So you'll all be part of the solution.

69
01:03:03,960 --> 01:03:06,000
So I've been at several economics conferences

70
01:03:06,000 --> 01:03:09,440
where economics professors get in front of an audience,

71
01:03:09,440 --> 01:03:12,480
and I've literally heard more than one person say,

72
01:03:12,480 --> 01:03:16,040
well, as we all know, economics assumes perfect information.

73
01:03:16,040 --> 01:03:17,040
Oh, god.

74
01:03:17,040 --> 01:03:18,920
And then I say, especially then, I look around

75
01:03:18,920 --> 01:03:20,760
and I see some journalists in the room.

76
01:03:20,760 --> 01:03:23,440
I'm like, what would any decent journalist be thinking?

77
01:03:23,440 --> 01:03:24,480
Wait a second.

78
01:03:24,480 --> 01:03:27,280
So you're a professor of a subject, right?

79
01:03:27,280 --> 01:03:30,200
And your subject assumes perfect information.

80
01:03:30,200 --> 01:03:31,640
You just said so.

81
01:03:31,640 --> 01:03:33,200
And I ought to trust you.

82
01:03:33,200 --> 01:03:36,120
This would be a very basic thing for you to get wrong.

83
01:03:36,120 --> 01:03:40,840
And when in the real world do we ever have perfect information?

84
01:03:40,840 --> 01:03:41,560
Never.

85
01:03:41,560 --> 01:03:44,720
So when does your subject describe the world?

86
01:03:44,720 --> 01:03:45,640
Never.

87
01:03:45,640 --> 01:03:47,160
So why do you exist?

88
01:03:47,160 --> 01:03:49,120
What is the point of this field?

89
01:03:49,120 --> 01:03:51,080
So to me, whenever I hear economists say this,

90
01:03:51,080 --> 01:03:53,720
I do want to curl up to a ball and either that

91
01:03:53,720 --> 01:03:54,760
or snap my arm up.

92
01:03:54,760 --> 01:03:56,720
Hey!

93
01:03:56,720 --> 01:03:57,600
Wrong, professor!

94
01:04:00,520 --> 01:04:03,080
So of course, it is absolutely untrue

95
01:04:03,080 --> 01:04:05,960
to say that economics assumes perfect information.

96
01:04:05,960 --> 01:04:08,400
There are some economic models with perfect information,

97
01:04:08,400 --> 01:04:11,520
but it doesn't mean the subject assumes perfect information.

98
01:04:11,520 --> 01:04:12,320
Of course not.

99
01:04:12,320 --> 01:04:13,880
That is ridiculous.

100
01:04:13,880 --> 01:04:16,120
There are multiple Nobel Prizes for economics

101
01:04:16,120 --> 01:04:18,480
with under uncertainty.

102
01:04:18,480 --> 01:04:21,240
So how could they possibly say something so wrong

103
01:04:21,240 --> 01:04:23,080
and don't know?

104
01:04:23,080 --> 01:04:25,160
Can't figure out what would possess a person

105
01:04:25,160 --> 01:04:28,400
to say something so ridiculous when they know better.

106
01:04:28,400 --> 01:04:33,040
I mean, it's kind of like, what about Akerlof?

107
01:04:33,040 --> 01:04:34,400
It's like, what do you mean?

108
01:04:34,400 --> 01:04:37,120
Well, he gave us some models of economic action

109
01:04:37,120 --> 01:04:40,000
under uncertainty, so what you said was wrong.

110
01:04:40,000 --> 01:04:41,920
Oh, yeah.

111
01:04:41,920 --> 01:04:44,920
Oh, yeah, economics assumes it, but some famous economists

112
01:04:44,920 --> 01:04:46,320
don't assume it.

113
01:04:46,320 --> 01:04:49,320
Oh, yeah, they develop poor careers doing it.

114
01:04:49,320 --> 01:04:53,160
So search theory is one very general model

115
01:04:53,160 --> 01:04:55,840
of economic action under uncertainty.

116
01:04:55,840 --> 01:04:59,880
So this came out, so originally, it was a stigma.

117
01:04:59,880 --> 01:05:03,000
And just for history of thought, this came out a year or two

118
01:05:03,000 --> 01:05:07,880
before Israel Kirzner did anything on entrepreneurship.

119
01:05:07,880 --> 01:05:11,640
So this is not a case where a Chicago economist was just

120
01:05:11,640 --> 01:05:13,840
formalizing what an Austrian had said.

121
01:05:13,840 --> 01:05:15,360
Far from it.

122
01:05:15,360 --> 01:05:18,920
So it's more a case of possibly when an Austrian eyes

123
01:05:18,920 --> 01:05:21,520
what a Chicago economist had said.

124
01:05:21,520 --> 01:05:22,960
Any case.

125
01:05:22,960 --> 01:05:24,760
So stringly general theory.

126
01:05:24,760 --> 01:05:27,800
It has the following basic assumptions.

127
01:05:27,800 --> 01:05:31,720
So first one we can call these just the assumption of effort,

128
01:05:31,720 --> 01:05:36,000
just as we put more time and effort into searching,

129
01:05:36,000 --> 01:05:37,600
increase your probability of finding

130
01:05:37,600 --> 01:05:39,440
whatever you're looking for.

131
01:05:39,440 --> 01:05:41,360
Pretty simple point.

132
01:05:41,360 --> 01:05:44,480
The next assumption, which is not absolutely theoretically

133
01:05:44,480 --> 01:05:47,320
vital, but is important in the real world,

134
01:05:47,320 --> 01:05:50,600
is to say that searching ability differs between people.

135
01:05:50,600 --> 01:05:53,000
It's like my mom can find something in her refrigerator

136
01:05:53,000 --> 01:05:54,480
that I could not find.

137
01:05:54,480 --> 01:05:57,800
At least she could take far less time for her to find something

138
01:05:57,800 --> 01:05:58,800
than I would take leave.

139
01:05:58,800 --> 01:06:01,400
So she has superior ability to search her own refrigerator

140
01:06:01,400 --> 01:06:02,840
than I do.

141
01:06:02,840 --> 01:06:08,160
And then third assumption, rational expectations.

142
01:06:08,160 --> 01:06:10,480
So this can be relaxed, of course.

143
01:06:10,480 --> 01:06:13,080
By standard model search theory, rational expectations

144
01:06:13,080 --> 01:06:14,200
is built in.

145
01:06:14,200 --> 01:06:16,760
So when people are trading off between the amount of time

146
01:06:16,760 --> 01:06:18,920
that they're going to put in studying or putting in

147
01:06:18,920 --> 01:06:27,040
searching, they are on average making the correct choice.

148
01:06:27,040 --> 01:06:28,800
And the main conclusion is that people

149
01:06:28,800 --> 01:06:32,120
keep searching until the expected marginal benefit

150
01:06:32,120 --> 01:06:35,520
of search is equal to the expected marginal cost.

151
01:06:35,520 --> 01:06:38,680
You can also add in a risk aversion adjustment if you want.

152
01:06:38,680 --> 01:06:41,440
That's the basic idea.

153
01:06:41,440 --> 01:06:43,400
So we say things like, I'm looking for my socks.

154
01:06:43,400 --> 01:06:44,680
First of all, if I put more effort

155
01:06:44,680 --> 01:06:47,280
in trying to find my socks, I'm more likely to find my socks.

156
01:06:47,280 --> 01:06:47,840
I don't know.

157
01:06:47,840 --> 01:06:49,840
I guess I'm in the plane.

158
01:06:49,840 --> 01:06:52,400
I was sitting next to a woman who lost an earring at the end.

159
01:06:52,400 --> 01:06:53,600
And then she conscripted my daughter

160
01:06:53,600 --> 01:06:55,920
to help with the search on the floor of the plane.

161
01:06:55,920 --> 01:06:58,120
She'd been nice, so we were perfectly happy to do it.

162
01:06:58,120 --> 01:07:00,160
So putting more effort.

163
01:07:00,160 --> 01:07:02,320
So you could just hope that you step on the earring

164
01:07:02,320 --> 01:07:03,820
and you'll find it, but she did not.

165
01:07:03,820 --> 01:07:05,640
So she's getting on her hands and knees

166
01:07:05,640 --> 01:07:08,640
and enlisting a small child to go and do the search,

167
01:07:08,640 --> 01:07:09,760
and help her.

168
01:07:09,760 --> 01:07:11,160
And then searching ability.

169
01:07:11,160 --> 01:07:12,880
So while my daughter's trying very hard,

170
01:07:12,880 --> 01:07:14,840
I think her actual ability to find her earring

171
01:07:14,840 --> 01:07:15,960
is pretty low.

172
01:07:15,960 --> 01:07:17,640
She might not know what an earring is.

173
01:07:17,640 --> 01:07:20,080
We want to be a little hazy on her anyway.

174
01:07:20,080 --> 01:07:21,880
And then finally, rationalizations.

175
01:07:21,880 --> 01:07:24,280
So she was saying, well, maybe it's just time to give up.

176
01:07:24,280 --> 01:07:26,360
Oh, no, there it is.

177
01:07:26,360 --> 01:07:28,680
But certainly while she was stuck on the plane,

178
01:07:28,680 --> 01:07:30,440
there was very little to lose in putting some more time

179
01:07:30,440 --> 01:07:31,960
into searching for this earring.

180
01:07:31,960 --> 01:07:33,040
I think she cared about it enough

181
01:07:33,040 --> 01:07:34,960
that she was going to wait for everyone to go and leave,

182
01:07:34,960 --> 01:07:36,560
and then do a more thorough search

183
01:07:36,560 --> 01:07:38,160
without other bodies around.

184
01:07:38,160 --> 01:07:41,380
But when she finally saw it right next to one of the screws

185
01:07:41,380 --> 01:07:43,360
that screws one of the seats down,

186
01:07:43,360 --> 01:07:45,360
it was very, very well hidden.

187
01:07:45,360 --> 01:07:46,800
But she kept saying, I know it's got to be

188
01:07:46,800 --> 01:07:48,160
around here somewhere.

189
01:07:48,160 --> 01:07:50,200
That sounds like rationalizations.

190
01:07:50,200 --> 01:07:51,920
It's got to be around here somewhere.

191
01:07:51,920 --> 01:07:53,560
Earrings don't just disappear.

192
01:07:53,560 --> 01:07:54,760
They don't run around.

193
01:07:54,760 --> 01:07:56,480
They don't bounce that far.

194
01:07:56,480 --> 01:07:59,000
They bounce once or twice.

195
01:07:59,000 --> 01:08:01,840
Now, there are endless applications of search theory.

196
01:08:01,840 --> 01:08:04,400
So there's things like doing R&D,

197
01:08:04,400 --> 01:08:08,240
so you're trying to find a cure for AIDS, for example.

198
01:08:08,240 --> 01:08:08,960
So effort.

199
01:08:08,960 --> 01:08:10,720
You're more likely to find a cure for AIDS

200
01:08:10,720 --> 01:08:12,800
if you actually start hiring some AIDS researchers

201
01:08:12,800 --> 01:08:15,400
and giving them some money and running experiments

202
01:08:15,400 --> 01:08:17,080
and testing things.

203
01:08:17,080 --> 01:08:20,000
So of course, it's possible that someone will just get lucky.

204
01:08:20,000 --> 01:08:22,120
Like the story of this discovery of penicillin.

205
01:08:22,120 --> 01:08:24,840
Someone just accidentally dropped two things together.

206
01:08:24,840 --> 01:08:26,560
You've got chocolate in my peanut butter.

207
01:08:26,560 --> 01:08:30,360
Now we have a cure for a bunch of bacterial infections.

208
01:08:30,360 --> 01:08:32,320
And then, of course, the difference is ability.

209
