01:18:49,400 --> 01:18:50,360
Yes, this is the king.

475
01:18:50,360 --> 01:18:50,860
Yeah.

476
01:18:50,860 --> 01:18:51,360
J.

477
01:18:51,360 --> 01:18:51,860
J.

478
01:18:51,860 --> 01:18:52,360
J.

479
01:18:52,360 --> 01:18:53,840
Oh, I didn't know you had a mark.

480
01:18:53,840 --> 01:18:55,080
I didn't know you had a mark.

481
01:18:55,080 --> 01:18:55,920
I just have a name.

482
01:18:55,920 --> 01:18:56,420
No, no.

483
01:18:56,420 --> 01:18:57,400
I won't discuss it.

484
01:18:57,400 --> 01:18:58,400
You will want to hear.

485
01:18:58,400 --> 01:18:59,900
Many of you will want to discuss it,

486
01:18:59,900 --> 01:19:02,360
but I find it's not a good idea to discuss it the same day

487
01:19:02,360 --> 01:19:03,360
that I did it last year.

488
01:19:03,360 --> 01:19:04,840
So starting on Thursday.

489
01:19:04,840 --> 01:19:06,000
I'm going to let you know.

490
01:19:06,000 --> 01:19:06,800
This is the king.

491
01:19:06,800 --> 01:19:19,280
So I'm now going to hand out homework three to you.

492
01:19:36,800 --> 01:19:38,800
I'm going to hand out homework three to you.


1
01:20:00,000 --> 01:20:02,700
All right.

2
01:20:03,660 --> 01:20:05,720
So, continuing with search theory.

3
01:20:05,720 --> 01:20:05,980
All right.

4
01:20:06,420 --> 01:20:11,500
Another example I like is rational amnesia.

5
01:20:11,900 --> 01:20:14,260
So, you ask your friend to pick you up from the airport

6
01:20:14,260 --> 01:20:15,060
and they don't show up.

7
01:20:15,060 --> 01:20:16,440
And then you say, what happened?

8
01:20:16,440 --> 01:20:17,260
And they say, I forgot.

9
01:20:18,480 --> 01:20:18,740
All right.

10
01:20:18,740 --> 01:20:22,240
Well, normally you do not find this a very good excuse.

11
01:20:22,240 --> 01:20:24,960
And, of course, the reason is that they could have put

12
01:20:24,960 --> 01:20:26,980
in some extra effort to not forget.

13
01:20:27,420 --> 01:20:28,740
They could have put it in their calendar.

14
01:20:28,740 --> 01:20:30,000
They could have set their alarm.

15
01:20:30,300 --> 01:20:32,060
They could have put a tattoo on their hand.

16
01:20:32,160 --> 01:20:34,200
Don't forget to pick up at this certain time.

17
01:20:34,820 --> 01:20:37,040
So, there are many different things that you can do in order

18
01:20:37,040 --> 01:20:39,340
to change the probability of forgetting.

19
01:20:40,020 --> 01:20:41,920
So, by putting in extra effort,

20
01:20:42,340 --> 01:20:45,060
you can raise the chance of remembering.

21
01:20:45,060 --> 01:20:46,720
Of course, there's different levels of ability.

22
01:20:46,720 --> 01:20:48,820
So, generally, older people have more trouble

23
01:20:48,820 --> 01:20:49,620
remembering things.

24
01:20:50,400 --> 01:20:51,440
So, there's that.

25
01:20:51,860 --> 01:20:53,440
And then, finally, rational expectations.

26
01:20:53,440 --> 01:20:56,140
You can also have rational expectations about your ability

27
01:20:56,140 --> 01:20:58,800
to remember things as well as to influence those probabilities.

28
01:20:58,800 --> 01:21:01,440
So, I will often, when I'm ordering for another person,

29
01:21:01,440 --> 01:21:03,620
order for the other person first before I forget what they want.

30
01:21:04,840 --> 01:21:07,580
Like if a kid says, I want to get chicken nuggets with,

31
01:21:08,200 --> 01:21:11,520
you know, without the spices and so on, then first thing order.

32
01:21:11,520 --> 01:21:11,720
All right.

33
01:21:11,720 --> 01:21:14,120
So, first, chicken nuggets without the spices,

34
01:21:14,120 --> 01:21:16,280
these things, and then that way I reduce my chance

35
01:21:16,280 --> 01:21:18,400
of forgetting because I know I'm likely to forget.

36
01:21:18,400 --> 01:21:20,280
I have a good idea about things that I tend to forget.

37
01:21:21,380 --> 01:21:21,600
All right.

38
01:21:21,600 --> 01:21:24,920
And on the other hand, of course, there's many things

39
01:21:24,920 --> 01:21:28,280
that I have not in any way worried that I might forget.

40
01:21:28,280 --> 01:21:30,540
So, normally I'm correct about those.

41
01:21:31,080 --> 01:21:32,560
So, there's things like you're watching a movie,

42
01:21:32,560 --> 01:21:33,720
you're like, have I seen this before?

43
01:21:33,720 --> 01:21:36,120
And it's like, well, no, this movie is so weird.

44
01:21:36,120 --> 01:21:37,500
I would remember it if I'd seen it.

45
01:21:37,500 --> 01:21:39,400
On the other hand, it's a very generic movie.

46
01:21:39,400 --> 01:21:40,580
Yeah, maybe I saw it before.

47
01:21:40,580 --> 01:21:41,960
I could easily forget a movie like this.

48
01:21:41,960 --> 01:21:42,640
It's very forgettable.

49
01:21:43,760 --> 01:21:43,960
All right.

50
01:21:43,960 --> 01:21:46,660
And there's that last one on comedy I will just skip.

51
01:21:46,660 --> 01:21:50,500
But general idea is part of the essence

52
01:21:50,500 --> 01:21:53,720
of comedy is something has to be surprising or unexpected.

53
01:21:53,720 --> 01:21:55,840
So, what comedians do is they look around

54
01:21:55,840 --> 01:21:58,040
for surprising funny observations to make.

55
01:21:58,440 --> 01:22:00,640
Many things are surprising but not funny, right?

56
01:22:00,640 --> 01:22:02,840
But in general, once you've heard a joke before a bunch

57
01:22:02,840 --> 01:22:05,320
of times and it ceases to be funny, so there's that.

58
01:22:05,320 --> 01:22:06,340
All right.

59
01:22:06,340 --> 01:22:10,840
Now, another application of search theory is what happens

60
01:22:10,840 --> 01:22:13,560
if there are some people who do not search very hard

61
01:22:13,560 --> 01:22:14,280
for a good price?

62
01:22:14,780 --> 01:22:17,140
For example, because the value of their time is very high.

63
01:22:18,040 --> 01:22:18,940
What occurs then?

64
01:22:19,300 --> 01:22:21,420
Well, if some people are not searching very hard

65
01:22:21,420 --> 01:22:23,180
for a good price if the value of their time is high,

66
01:22:23,180 --> 01:22:26,960
then they are likely to go and take the first offer

67
01:22:26,960 --> 01:22:28,240
that anyone offers to them.

68
01:22:28,240 --> 01:22:31,120
And in that case, if you can find those people

69
01:22:31,120 --> 01:22:33,440
who are very busy and offer them what they want,

70
01:22:33,880 --> 01:22:36,540
you have a chance of making a very high profit.

71
01:22:37,300 --> 01:22:39,860
So, for example, when I was a tourist in Istanbul,

72
01:22:40,860 --> 01:22:41,700
I didn't sell you.

73
01:22:41,700 --> 01:22:42,680
I only had a few hours.

74
01:22:42,680 --> 01:22:44,640
I wasn't going to go shopping around for souvenirs.

75
01:22:45,100 --> 01:22:47,440
But it didn't matter because souvenirs literally came to me.

76
01:22:47,440 --> 01:22:49,800
People would just say, you, you're a tourist, right?

77
01:22:49,800 --> 01:22:51,320
And they would come up and say,

78
01:22:51,320 --> 01:22:52,820
I have a bunch of different products here.

79
01:22:52,820 --> 01:22:54,820
And they actually have like an overcoat where they would go

80
01:22:54,820 --> 01:22:57,020
and display their wares, right?

81
01:22:57,020 --> 01:22:59,300
And if you said, I want something like that,

82
01:22:59,300 --> 01:23:00,220
and they'd stand right here.

83
01:23:00,220 --> 01:23:01,300
And they'd go running, and they're like,

84
01:23:01,300 --> 01:23:02,020
where are you going?

85
01:23:02,020 --> 01:23:03,380
And come back, here's what you want.

86
01:23:03,900 --> 01:23:04,740
Okay. All right.

87
01:23:04,740 --> 01:23:08,740
So, and what they're doing is they are making all the effort

88
01:23:08,740 --> 01:23:11,300
to search for people who would want to buy the stuff

89
01:23:11,840 --> 01:23:14,120
because the people that want to buy it have a very high value

90
01:23:14,120 --> 01:23:15,300
time, so they're not going to search.

91
01:23:15,300 --> 01:23:17,540
So then it gets naturally outsourced to other people.

92
01:23:17,540 --> 01:23:23,420
And search theory also gives us an economic way

93
01:23:23,420 --> 01:23:25,840
of understanding what it means for something

94
01:23:25,840 --> 01:23:27,380
to be overpriced or underpriced.

95
01:23:28,120 --> 01:23:29,740
Right? Because in supply and demand terms,

96
01:23:29,740 --> 01:23:31,260
it doesn't really make sense if something is overpriced

97
01:23:31,260 --> 01:23:31,900
or underpriced.

98
01:23:32,380 --> 01:23:35,540
If it's available for sale at all, then you can't go

99
01:23:35,540 --> 01:23:38,020
and charge extra because no one will buy it.

100
01:23:38,020 --> 01:23:40,620
And if you offer for less, then you're just giving away money.

101
01:23:40,620 --> 01:23:41,820
So why would anybody do that?

102
01:23:42,380 --> 01:23:44,320
But with search theory, this actually opens

103
01:23:44,320 --> 01:23:48,420
up this common sense possibility that you're getting a really good

104
01:23:48,420 --> 01:23:49,500
deal or a really bad deal.

105
01:23:50,680 --> 01:23:53,700
Okay. So, you know, one example of this.

106
01:23:53,700 --> 01:23:56,620
So, once you have been working at a job for a while,

107
01:23:56,620 --> 01:23:59,120
it is common that headhunters will call you.

108
01:23:59,580 --> 01:24:03,980
A headhunter is a professional job searcher who calls up people

109
01:24:03,980 --> 01:24:05,660
and tries to steal you away from your current firm.

110
01:24:06,860 --> 01:24:07,080
All right.

111
01:24:07,080 --> 01:24:11,100
Now, why in the world would they be trying to go and call you up

112
01:24:11,100 --> 01:24:12,840
and steal you away from your current job?

113
01:24:12,840 --> 01:24:15,240
Well, first of all, like why would you even listen to them?

114
01:24:15,240 --> 01:24:15,340
Yeah.

115
01:24:15,340 --> 01:24:18,340
Because there's a possibility

116
01:24:18,340 --> 01:24:23,460
that you're currently underpaid.

117
01:24:23,460 --> 01:24:26,600
Right? And why do they make the effort to find you?

118
01:24:26,600 --> 01:24:30,900
Yes. Because even after you move, they still think you're

119
01:24:30,900 --> 01:24:31,900
going to be underpaid.

120
01:24:32,760 --> 01:24:36,660
Right? So the fact that you are having a lot of trouble going

121
01:24:36,660 --> 01:24:38,840
or that people are hunting you down and trying

122
01:24:38,840 --> 01:24:41,600
to offer you a job, it's a sign that you're underpaid

123
01:24:41,600 --> 01:24:44,360
and it's a way, and it's a why haven't you solved your

124
01:24:44,360 --> 01:24:44,960
own problem.

125
01:24:45,260 --> 01:24:48,280
Maybe the value of your time is so high that for you to go

126
01:24:48,280 --> 01:24:49,140
and spend the time looking

127
01:24:49,140 --> 01:24:51,480
for another job would actually not be worth it,

128
01:24:51,480 --> 01:24:53,240
but it would be worth it to outsource to somebody else.

129
01:24:53,880 --> 01:24:56,220
There is a Great Simpsons episode

130
01:24:56,220 --> 01:24:58,920
where there's the James Bond villain who's starting

131
01:24:58,920 --> 01:25:01,120
up a new nuclear power plant for world domination.

132
01:25:01,440 --> 01:25:03,880
And it begins by him trying to hire away Smithers.

133
01:25:04,300 --> 01:25:06,600
And he like follows him down the road in a car saying, hey,

134
01:25:06,600 --> 01:25:07,640
do you want to work for me?

135
01:25:07,640 --> 01:25:08,840
He's like, no, thank you.

136
01:25:09,120 --> 01:25:10,200
No, thank you, spoken for.

137
01:25:10,200 --> 01:25:12,420
And then finally Smithers gets so annoyed that he says,

138
01:25:12,760 --> 01:25:14,620
can't a man walk down the street in America

139
01:25:14,620 --> 01:25:15,980
without being offered a job?

140
01:25:18,460 --> 01:25:20,540
So that is a possibility.

141
01:25:21,280 --> 01:25:23,980
Now on the other hand, when you're looking for a first job,

142
01:25:23,980 --> 01:25:26,440
that's when you usually have exactly the opposite experience

143
01:25:26,920 --> 01:25:29,040
where no one is calling you to offer you a job.

144
01:25:29,340 --> 01:25:31,160
Instead, you are trying to reach

145
01:25:31,160 --> 01:25:34,480
out to every possible person you can in order to get a job.

146
01:25:34,480 --> 01:25:36,400
And most of the time people are just ignoring you,

147
01:25:36,400 --> 01:25:38,500
not returning your calls, slamming doors in your face.

148
01:25:38,500 --> 01:25:40,540
So this is what we call hitting the pavement where you have

149
01:25:40,540 --> 01:25:42,880
to go and do a bunch of really discouraging interviews,

150
01:25:43,200 --> 01:25:44,260
face a lot of rejection.

151
01:25:44,840 --> 01:25:47,960
So and the search theoretic story here is

152
01:25:47,960 --> 01:25:51,200
that headhunters arise when you are underpaid.

153
01:25:52,080 --> 01:25:53,760
On the other hand, people put all this effort

154
01:25:53,760 --> 01:25:55,280
into finding a job when you're overpaid.

155
01:25:55,780 --> 01:25:59,840
When the job is actually really too good for you and the amount

156
01:25:59,840 --> 01:26:01,200
of money they're paying you is actually more

157
01:26:01,200 --> 01:26:01,880
than you're really worth,

158
01:26:01,880 --> 01:26:04,560
that's when people will put an enormous amount of effort

159
01:26:04,560 --> 01:26:08,040
to find a job because it's great if you can find

160
01:26:08,040 --> 01:26:09,980
that job because it's really more than you deserve.

161
01:26:10,840 --> 01:26:13,420
So I remember when my wife was trying to get her first job

162
01:26:13,420 --> 01:26:16,660
in law school and I say, wow, like clearly the wages

163
01:26:16,660 --> 01:26:18,820
for inexperienced lawyers were way too high.

164
01:26:19,440 --> 01:26:20,780
It would be far better if they were way lower

165
01:26:20,780 --> 01:26:22,540
because then it wouldn't be so hard to get a job.

166
01:26:23,220 --> 01:26:24,840
Right? So she didn't appreciate that so much.

167
01:26:24,840 --> 01:26:30,200
But then, you know, once she actually had a job, it didn't take

168
01:26:30,200 --> 01:26:32,320
that many months before she starts getting phone calls

169
01:26:32,320 --> 01:26:33,600
from headhunters and I said, see,

170
01:26:34,000 --> 01:26:35,680
now you're on the other side of the story.

171
01:26:35,680 --> 01:26:38,280
Now you're on the side of the market

172
01:26:38,360 --> 01:26:40,320
where you're actually paid less than you're worth.

173
01:26:41,360 --> 01:26:43,100
Right? Now this does raise interesting questions.

174
01:26:43,520 --> 01:26:46,800
Why aren't wages more carefully matched to current productivity?

175
01:26:47,380 --> 01:26:50,440
Like it's very standard that you get a raise once a year,

176
01:26:50,820 --> 01:26:53,000
but your productivity in your 12th month of working

177
01:26:53,000 --> 01:26:55,760
as a lawyer is far higher than your productivity

178
01:26:55,760 --> 01:26:57,080
in your first month as a lawyer.

179
01:26:57,440 --> 01:26:59,260
Your first month you probably have highly negative

180
01:26:59,260 --> 01:27:01,960
productivity because you don't know how to do anything

181
01:27:02,280 --> 01:27:04,720
and you interrupt people who do know how to do stuff

182
01:27:04,720 --> 01:27:06,100
who could be making money.

183
01:27:06,680 --> 01:27:10,020
So in that case, you know, so there is this puzzle

184
01:27:10,020 --> 01:27:10,820
about what's going on.

185
01:27:10,960 --> 01:27:11,040
But.

186
01:27:11,540 --> 01:27:11,740
I don't know.

187
01:27:11,740 --> 01:27:12,840
Could it just be like metering costs?

188
01:27:13,220 --> 01:27:13,680
I don't know.

189
01:27:13,680 --> 01:27:15,580
Metering costs are pretty high.

190
01:27:15,580 --> 01:27:18,140
Yeah, it could be, but like what would be so hard

191
01:27:18,140 --> 01:27:19,820
with just saying, look, here's the formula.

192
01:27:20,500 --> 01:27:22,220
Like every month that you have here, you know,

193
01:27:22,220 --> 01:27:25,460
it's like the wage adjusts monthly instead of yearly.

194
01:27:25,460 --> 01:27:27,140
That's what an apprenticeship used to be like.

195
01:27:27,500 --> 01:27:28,260
You pay to work.

196
01:27:28,780 --> 01:27:29,700
You learn.

197
01:27:30,540 --> 01:27:31,860
And then at some point you get paid.

198
01:27:32,060 --> 01:27:33,060
Right. Although even there,

199
01:27:33,060 --> 01:27:35,020
there's the weird non-continuity.

200
01:27:35,020 --> 01:27:36,560
There's a discontinuity of it.

201
01:27:37,020 --> 01:27:38,580
Where there's a period where you're paying

202
01:27:38,580 --> 01:27:41,460
and then do your magic.

203
01:27:41,720 --> 01:27:45,680
And then suddenly now you should be paid.

204
01:27:45,680 --> 01:27:47,540
When it would seem like there's a little difference

205
01:27:47,540 --> 01:27:49,160
in the day before.

206
01:27:49,160 --> 01:27:50,380
The last day of the apprenticeship

207
01:27:50,380 --> 01:27:51,460
and the first day of your job,

208
01:27:51,780 --> 01:27:53,500
you seem like you're virtually an equivalent worker

209
01:27:53,500 --> 01:27:55,080
and yet the market treats you very differently.

210
01:27:55,080 --> 01:27:55,180
Yes.

211
01:27:55,180 --> 01:28:03,360
Yes. Economic theory comedy.

212
01:28:03,360 --> 01:28:04,160
No, there's no literature.

213
01:28:04,160 --> 01:28:05,140
It's just an example for me.

214
01:28:05,140 --> 01:28:05,560
Yes. Yes.

215
01:28:05,560 --> 01:28:16,280
I mean, it probably wasn't in terms of search theory though.

216
01:28:16,280 --> 01:28:19,800
It's probably in terms of Smithian psychology,

217
01:28:19,800 --> 01:28:22,240
which is connected, but not quite the same.

218
01:28:23,080 --> 01:28:23,320
All right.

219
01:28:23,320 --> 01:28:25,180
Anyway, here's my plan.

220
01:28:25,260 --> 01:28:27,620
If the economics of perfect information does not make sense,

221
01:28:27,620 --> 01:28:29,160
then at least try search theory.

222
01:28:29,940 --> 01:28:32,500
And it explains almost everything else.

223
01:28:33,100 --> 01:28:36,980
So there's a common sense idea that it's much harder

224
01:28:36,980 --> 01:28:38,480
to get a job than to buy a loaf of bread.

225
01:28:39,700 --> 01:28:42,100
And if this isn't true, what is?

226
01:28:42,140 --> 01:28:43,860
Of course it's way harder to get a job

227
01:28:43,860 --> 01:28:44,820
than to buy a loaf of bread.

228
01:28:45,580 --> 01:28:48,060
Right. And the search theoretic answer, of course,

229
01:28:48,060 --> 01:28:49,560
is for buying a loaf of bread,

230
01:28:49,560 --> 01:28:51,080
there's very little search involved.

231
01:28:51,580 --> 01:28:52,840
Right. There's the store.

232
01:28:52,840 --> 01:28:53,860
I know where the store is.

233
01:28:54,360 --> 01:28:55,860
I may not know the aisle, but I just look

234
01:28:55,860 --> 01:28:56,820
up at the aisle numbers.

235
01:28:56,820 --> 01:28:57,420
There's the bread.

236
01:28:57,420 --> 01:28:58,900
I go there and I buy a loaf of bread.

237
01:28:58,900 --> 01:29:00,660
And you offer some money and they don't say, well,

238
01:29:00,660 --> 01:29:01,600
what kind of money is this?

239
01:29:01,600 --> 01:29:03,340
The same kind of money everyone else is using.

240
01:29:03,700 --> 01:29:06,520
So there's no big issue.

241
01:29:06,520 --> 01:29:07,640
You know what the bread is.

242
01:29:07,640 --> 01:29:08,800
They know what the money is.

243
01:29:09,060 --> 01:29:11,360
It's easy to put buyer and seller together.

244
01:29:11,660 --> 01:29:13,340
So buying a loaf of bread is easy.

245
01:29:13,780 --> 01:29:15,240
On the other hand, if you want to get a job,

