01:33:42,500 --> 01:33:43,360
So moving on.

361
01:33:44,220 --> 01:33:44,520
All right.

362
01:33:44,600 --> 01:33:46,360
So measures of risk aversion.

363
01:33:47,680 --> 01:33:47,820
All right.

364
01:33:48,180 --> 01:33:51,300
So remember we started with expected value maximization

365
01:33:51,660 --> 01:33:53,340
and expected utility maximization

366
01:33:53,340 --> 01:33:54,800
as an improved theory.

367
01:33:55,700 --> 01:33:59,920
So you could imagine a theory of economic action under uncertainty

368
01:34:00,460 --> 01:34:03,680
where all that everybody does is maximize the expected value

369
01:34:03,680 --> 01:34:04,540
of whatever they do.

370
01:34:05,280 --> 01:34:08,580
So in that case, if you have a one in a million chance

371
01:34:08,580 --> 01:34:12,520
of a trillion dollars, or you could have $100,000 for sure,

372
01:34:12,860 --> 01:34:14,840
then everyone would go for the one in a million chance

373
01:34:14,840 --> 01:34:16,820
of a trillion dollars because that is a higher expected value.

374
01:34:18,160 --> 01:34:18,440
All right.

375
01:34:18,860 --> 01:34:22,040
So. And if your intuition is, yeah, I would totally go

376
01:34:22,040 --> 01:34:23,700
for that one in a million chance of a trillion dollars,

377
01:34:23,700 --> 01:34:25,440
then this might make sense to you.

378
01:34:25,440 --> 01:34:28,060
But most people don't think that way.

379
01:34:28,600 --> 01:34:31,080
And most people don't think that most people think that way.

380
01:34:31,080 --> 01:34:34,720
So that's where we bring in expected utility maximization

381
01:34:35,180 --> 01:34:39,060
as an improved theory that can explain people's reluctance

382
01:34:39,060 --> 01:34:42,600
to go and take these extreme long shots

383
01:34:42,600 --> 01:34:43,800
with high expected value.

384
01:34:43,800 --> 01:34:43,900
All right.

385
01:34:43,900 --> 01:34:47,760
So anyway, those are the cases of risk.

386
01:34:48,260 --> 01:34:51,700
Now suppose that you choose between X dollars

387
01:34:52,500 --> 01:34:57,020
with a probability of one, or you're going to have X over Q

388
01:34:57,020 --> 01:35:00,000
dollars with a probability of P equal to Q.

389
01:35:00,000 --> 01:35:01,120
All right.

390
01:35:01,940 --> 01:35:06,300
Now a simple taxonomy that economists use is

391
01:35:06,300 --> 01:35:09,020
if you're indifferent between the sure thing

392
01:35:09,020 --> 01:35:10,640
and the gamble, then you are risk neutral.

393
01:35:12,280 --> 01:35:12,500
All right.

394
01:35:12,620 --> 01:35:15,760
If you prefer the sure thing to gamble, you are risk averse.

395
01:35:16,600 --> 01:35:18,060
And if you prefer the gamble to the sure thing,

396
01:35:18,060 --> 01:35:18,820
you are risk preferring.

397
01:35:19,440 --> 01:35:22,240
Non-economists often say risk averse

398
01:35:22,240 --> 01:35:24,580
when what they mean is low expected value.

399
01:35:26,440 --> 01:35:28,460
So and I've even heard people say, well, of course,

400
01:35:28,460 --> 01:35:28,980
I'm risk averse.

401
01:35:28,980 --> 01:35:30,900
Who wouldn't be risk averse because I'd rather not have the

402
01:35:30,900 --> 01:35:31,760
risk than have the risk.

403
01:35:32,720 --> 01:35:33,940
That's not what risk aversion means.

404
01:35:34,620 --> 01:35:37,300
So risk aversion does not mean that you would rather get rid

405
01:35:37,300 --> 01:35:38,520
of a risk at no cost.

406
01:35:39,080 --> 01:35:40,980
Rather, it means that if you have a choice

407
01:35:40,980 --> 01:35:44,440
between two gambles and one is,

408
01:35:44,440 --> 01:35:46,140
and they both have the same expected value,

409
01:35:46,140 --> 01:35:48,920
but one is certain and one is not, risk aversion means

410
01:35:48,920 --> 01:35:52,420
that you strictly prefer the one that is certain to the one

411
01:35:52,420 --> 01:35:53,440
that is not.

412
01:35:53,440 --> 01:35:55,480
So the expected values are the same,

413
01:35:55,800 --> 01:35:58,060
but of course the actual distributions are different,

414
01:35:58,060 --> 01:36:01,200
right, because the certainty has certainty of one outcome

415
01:36:01,200 --> 01:36:03,100
and the one where it's not certain means there's a range

416
01:36:03,100 --> 01:36:03,720
of possibilities.

417
01:36:04,980 --> 01:36:06,180
Okay. All right.

418
01:36:06,180 --> 01:36:10,040
Now most economic models assume that actors are risk averse.

419
01:36:10,880 --> 01:36:13,000
It's a standard to assume that actors are risk averse.

420
01:36:13,900 --> 01:36:14,880
So actors.

421
01:36:15,340 --> 01:36:16,640
Although it's also very common

422
01:36:16,640 --> 01:36:18,940
to assume that firms are risk neutral.

423
01:36:19,620 --> 01:36:24,340
It is abnormal to ever assume that anyone's risk preferring,

424
01:36:24,340 --> 01:36:25,160
although you can do it.

425
01:36:25,160 --> 01:36:28,580
But normally we think of individual people

426
01:36:28,580 --> 01:36:30,880
as being risk averse and firms as being risk neutral.

427
01:36:31,440 --> 01:36:33,280
Why would firms be risk neutral?

428
01:36:33,620 --> 01:36:36,520
Common story is well, since the shareholders can diversify,

429
01:36:37,080 --> 01:36:40,240
no one needs to actually have a lot of stock in any one company,

430
01:36:40,580 --> 01:36:43,380
in which case there's no reason to worry about them gambling.

431
01:36:43,820 --> 01:36:45,300
It's long and you just want them to go

432
01:36:45,300 --> 01:36:47,780
and do the highest expected value thing.

433
01:36:48,500 --> 01:36:49,700
There's a lot of evidence that even

434
01:36:49,700 --> 01:36:50,920
if the firms are not risk neutral,

435
01:36:50,920 --> 01:36:53,560
or firms are actually not risk neutral, but there is a story

436
01:36:53,560 --> 01:36:55,220
about why firms would be different, which is

437
01:36:55,220 --> 01:36:58,720
that you can diversify away from the risk.

438
01:36:59,980 --> 01:37:04,720
Okay. Right now, if you are graphing these possibilities,

439
01:37:05,360 --> 01:37:08,940
right, if you're graphing these possibilities, then if we go

440
01:37:08,940 --> 01:37:11,460
and draw our utility wealth function,

441
01:37:12,380 --> 01:37:14,360
if you draw a utility wealth function,

442
01:37:14,760 --> 01:37:19,220
then a risk averse agent has a concave utility wealth function,

443
01:37:19,220 --> 01:37:24,620
which means that if you draw a line, you draw a line

444
01:37:25,180 --> 01:37:27,580
between any two points on the utility, between any two points,

445
01:37:28,020 --> 01:37:29,480
then the utility function is always going

446
01:37:29,480 --> 01:37:31,260
to be above that line.

447
01:37:31,920 --> 01:37:34,400
What that means is that you would prefer something

448
01:37:34,720 --> 01:37:37,380
with certainty, with the same expected value,

449
01:37:37,620 --> 01:37:39,560
to either of the more extreme endpoints.

450
01:37:40,020 --> 01:37:41,700
So saying, look, you could either that or that,

451
01:37:42,280 --> 01:37:45,940
but this line here between them, that shows a whole range

452
01:37:45,940 --> 01:37:49,760
of combinations with the same expected value,

453
01:37:49,760 --> 01:37:50,940
and this says that you're always going to,

454
01:37:50,940 --> 01:37:52,680
with your risk averse, you always prefer

455
01:37:53,020 --> 01:37:57,180
to have the intermediate result, the average result,

456
01:37:57,600 --> 01:38:00,820
with certainty, than to take the gamble of the possible endpoints.

457
01:38:02,220 --> 01:38:05,300
Okay. So, and you might remember the formula for a line,

458
01:38:05,300 --> 01:38:10,800
so you've got AX plus 1 minus AY, right, is always preferred

459
01:38:10,800 --> 01:38:12,900
to the gamble of X with probability A,

460
01:38:12,900 --> 01:38:16,960
and the line on the right hand Y with probability 1 minus A.

461
01:38:17,400 --> 01:38:18,240
Now, on the other hand, of course,

462
01:38:18,240 --> 01:38:21,080
if you have this utility function, then the line is

463
01:38:21,080 --> 01:38:24,020
above the utility function, and that says that people,

464
01:38:24,020 --> 01:38:28,240
and that says that actually a certain thing is not

465
01:38:28,240 --> 01:38:30,300
what people want, a certain thing is not what people want,

466
01:38:30,380 --> 01:38:32,320
people actually want to have the other way around, right,

467
01:38:32,320 --> 01:38:35,260
and then if it is a straight line, a straight line,

468
01:38:35,260 --> 01:38:36,920
that means that you are indifferent

469
01:38:37,360 --> 01:38:40,060
between the gamble and the certainty.

470
01:38:40,060 --> 01:38:43,020
Okay. All right.

471
01:38:43,020 --> 01:38:44,240
Now, this brings us to the concept

472
01:38:44,240 --> 01:38:46,960
of certainty equivalence, which says that if you're indifferent

473
01:38:46,960 --> 01:38:49,500
between a gamble and X star with certainty,

474
01:38:50,080 --> 01:38:52,440
then X star is the gamble certainty equivalence.

475
01:38:53,400 --> 01:38:54,960
So, in other words, imagine a gamble,

476
01:38:54,960 --> 01:38:57,540
it could be any gamble at all, and then say, right,

477
01:38:57,680 --> 01:39:01,180
what exact amount of money would you pay to own that gamble?

478
01:39:02,540 --> 01:39:04,360
What exact money would you pay to own that gamble?

479
01:39:04,600 --> 01:39:05,980
What amount of money would make you indifferent,

480
01:39:05,980 --> 01:39:07,940
for if you either have that amount of money for sure,

481
01:39:07,940 --> 01:39:10,160
or the gamble, what amount would make you indifferent,

482
01:39:10,280 --> 01:39:12,220
and that would be a dollar value.

483
01:39:12,940 --> 01:39:14,180
So, you could say, all right, look,

484
01:39:14,180 --> 01:39:17,860
if I could either face the expected risk of deportation,

485
01:39:18,480 --> 01:39:20,760
right, or I could just have a certain,

486
01:39:20,760 --> 01:39:23,400
or I could just pay a certain fixed amount of money

487
01:39:23,400 --> 01:39:27,420
to be left alone for sure, right, how much money would I pay

488
01:39:27,420 --> 01:39:29,240
in order to get rid of that danger, that worry?

489
01:39:30,900 --> 01:39:32,540
Okay. All right.

