4
01:60:09,160 --> 01:60:12,240
All right, anyway, so a general point is this.

5
01:60:12,240 --> 01:60:15,080
So just because you're ignorant doesn't mean you're stupid.

6
01:60:15,080 --> 01:60:17,000
So if you are uncertain,

7
01:60:17,000 --> 01:60:19,000
you can adopt more general purpose strategies

8
01:60:19,000 --> 01:60:21,640
to take account of all the possible outcomes.

9
01:60:21,640 --> 01:60:22,960
Right, so like with search theory.

10
01:60:22,960 --> 01:60:26,240
Yes, you don't know what every possible employer wants,

11
01:60:26,240 --> 01:60:28,520
but you can still form general strategies

12
01:60:28,520 --> 01:60:31,600
about to figure out what in general would people like.

13
01:60:31,600 --> 01:60:34,040
So maybe there's some employers that like people

14
01:60:34,040 --> 01:60:37,600
who don't dress in suits, but on average they do.

15
01:60:37,600 --> 01:60:40,000
So the natural thing to do is to go and dress up

16
01:60:40,000 --> 01:60:43,120
in a way where that will actually be pleasing

17
01:60:43,120 --> 01:60:45,080
to market overall, which might mean

18
01:60:45,080 --> 01:60:46,520
that you lose out on some opportunities,

19
01:60:46,520 --> 01:60:48,320
but on average you're still playing the odds

20
01:60:48,320 --> 01:60:49,320
and doing your best.

21
01:60:50,940 --> 01:60:53,000
And furthermore, there are actually our theorems saying

22
01:60:53,000 --> 01:60:55,180
that as long as information is symmetric,

23
01:60:55,180 --> 01:60:57,240
then there is in fact no welfare loss.

24
01:60:57,240 --> 01:60:59,240
So any textbook that just says that perfect information

25
01:60:59,240 --> 01:61:02,200
in general leads to suboptimal outcomes is wrong.

26
01:61:02,200 --> 01:61:04,600
It's just grossly overstating the result.

