01:71:59,320 --> 01:72:01,880
at a reasonable speed, and this garage door

304
01:72:01,880 --> 01:72:02,880
would not be destroyed.

305
01:72:02,880 --> 01:72:05,480
So, you can do it that way.

306
01:72:05,480 --> 01:72:07,680
All right, so anyway, so if you can perfectly infer

307
01:72:07,680 --> 01:72:10,480
behavior from outcomes, then information asymmetries

308
01:72:10,480 --> 01:72:14,400
don't make very little difference, really no difference,

309
01:72:14,400 --> 01:72:17,760
because there's a bunch of things that you don't know,

310
01:72:17,760 --> 01:72:19,360
that there's asymmetric information,

311
01:72:19,360 --> 01:72:23,360
but you know enough to figure out what happened,

312
01:72:23,360 --> 01:72:25,200
and as long as you know enough to figure out what happened,

313
01:72:25,200 --> 01:72:26,240
the fact that you don't know everything

314
01:72:26,240 --> 01:72:27,280
isn't very important.

315
01:72:28,160 --> 01:72:29,880
Okay, all right.

316
01:72:30,760 --> 01:72:32,840
But inferences from behavior and outcomes

317
01:72:32,840 --> 01:72:35,160
are, in the real world, of course, less than perfect,

318
01:72:35,160 --> 01:72:37,240
so the moral hazard problem does persist to some degree.

319
01:72:37,240 --> 01:72:39,480
So, you know, maybe the person driving the car

320
01:72:39,480 --> 01:72:42,160
had a sudden blackout, and they've never had a blackout

321
01:72:42,160 --> 01:72:44,760
in their life before, so they had no way of knowing

322
01:72:44,760 --> 01:72:45,920
that there would ever be a blackout,

323
01:72:45,920 --> 01:72:47,120
and that's what happened.

324
01:72:47,120 --> 01:72:48,680
All right, so that could occur.

325
01:72:49,640 --> 01:72:52,680
All right, now moral hazard is not an efficiency problem

326
01:72:52,680 --> 01:72:54,640
if the agents are risk neutral, of course.

327
01:72:54,640 --> 01:72:57,800
Then you just live, then you don't have any insurance,

328
01:72:57,800 --> 01:72:59,800
and you just let people live with the risk,

329
01:72:59,800 --> 01:73:00,920
and if people live with the risk,

330
01:73:00,920 --> 01:73:03,480
then there's not gonna be any issue.

331
01:73:03,480 --> 01:73:06,280
So imagine a risk neutral CEO.

332
01:73:07,240 --> 01:73:09,680
There is a really simple way that you can go

333
01:73:09,680 --> 01:73:11,160
and handle the problem of good incentives

334
01:73:11,160 --> 01:73:13,440
to the risk neutral CEO, and then sell them

335
01:73:13,440 --> 01:73:14,320
the whole company.

336
01:73:14,320 --> 01:73:18,400
So you turn the company into a sole proprietorship,

337
01:73:18,400 --> 01:73:21,840
and then, once you own the entire thing,

338
01:73:21,840 --> 01:73:25,120
if it is, you know, the only reason why you wouldn't go

339
01:73:25,120 --> 01:73:26,360
and put in a million dollars worth of,

340
01:73:26,360 --> 01:73:28,320
or if there's something that will make the company

341
01:73:28,320 --> 01:73:30,560
worth a million more, the only reason why you wouldn't

342
01:73:30,560 --> 01:73:32,720
put in the effort to do that is if the effort

343
01:73:32,720 --> 01:73:37,720
subjectively costs you more than, what?

344
01:73:39,840 --> 01:73:42,360
More than, yeah, so like, you know, you get out of bed,

345
01:73:42,360 --> 01:73:44,280
and you can save the company a million dollars.

346
01:73:44,280 --> 01:73:46,040
Why don't you get out of bed if you're the sole owner

347
01:73:46,040 --> 01:73:47,040
of the company?

348
01:73:47,040 --> 01:73:48,200
Staying in bed's worth more than a million dollars.

349
01:73:48,200 --> 01:73:50,360
Yeah, staying in bed's worth more than a million dollars.

350
01:73:50,360 --> 01:73:51,200
Could that ever be?

351
01:73:51,200 --> 01:73:53,760
I mean, once you're a billionaire, I would imagine.

352
01:73:53,760 --> 01:73:55,120
Yeah, seems like it.

353
01:73:55,120 --> 01:73:57,520
Okay, all right, so the idea is then you would exert

354
01:73:57,520 --> 01:73:59,960
management efforts if and only if the expected gain

355
01:73:59,960 --> 01:74:02,360
exceeds the expected effort cost.

356
01:74:02,360 --> 01:74:05,320
So, and in Landsberg, he talks about the problem

357
01:74:05,320 --> 01:74:08,080
of the moral hazard involved in splitting a check.

358
01:74:08,080 --> 01:74:10,760
Right, so you go out to dinner with 10 people,

359
01:74:10,760 --> 01:74:12,160
you don't even like each other very much,

360
01:74:12,160 --> 01:74:14,200
just to make the problem more interesting,

361
01:74:14,200 --> 01:74:17,280
and then there's the question of how do we split the check?

362
01:74:17,280 --> 01:74:20,080
Well, the person, like one convenient answer is

363
01:74:20,080 --> 01:74:22,520
we all just go and put in an equal amount.

364
01:74:23,520 --> 01:74:25,600
The problem with that, of course, especially if people

365
01:74:25,600 --> 01:74:27,560
know that's gonna be the rule,

366
01:74:27,560 --> 01:74:28,600
suppose you know that's the rule,

367
01:74:28,600 --> 01:74:30,680
then what does this lead everybody to do?

368
01:74:30,680 --> 01:74:31,520
Free ride.

369
01:74:31,520 --> 01:74:33,200
Free ride, how?

370
01:74:33,200 --> 01:74:35,640
Order expensive, order lots of expensive stuff.

371
01:74:35,640 --> 01:74:38,120
So yeah, there's that approach.

372
01:74:38,120 --> 01:74:41,000
Of course, the opposite end is have everybody go

373
01:74:41,000 --> 01:74:42,960
and pay for exactly what they paid for,

374
01:74:42,960 --> 01:74:45,800
so everyone pays based upon exactly what they bought,

375
01:74:45,800 --> 01:74:47,880
but that requires a very extensive monitoring,

376
01:74:47,880 --> 01:74:50,440
that seems like, that's pretty hard, that's quite hard.

377
01:74:50,440 --> 01:74:53,560
And then Landsberg comes up with a really weird contract.

378
01:74:53,560 --> 01:74:56,520
Here's the contract where everyone pays the full check.

379
01:74:57,600 --> 01:75:00,320
So that's computationally very simple, right?

380
01:75:00,320 --> 01:75:03,960
Everyone pays, if the group check comes to $800,

381
01:75:03,960 --> 01:75:06,480
everyone pays $800.

382
01:75:06,480 --> 01:75:08,960
Why would anyone go to a restaurant where everyone

383
01:75:08,960 --> 01:75:11,400
has to pay the full check if they're in a group?

384
01:75:11,400 --> 01:75:13,280
And Landsberg's answer is, well,

385
01:75:13,280 --> 01:75:15,720
restaurants would pay you to come in.

386
01:75:15,720 --> 01:75:17,720
Restaurants would actually go and say,

387
01:75:17,720 --> 01:75:22,720
look, here is a $770 voucher for every person who comes in,

388
01:75:24,840 --> 01:75:26,960
as long as you're part of a group of 10 people

389
01:75:26,960 --> 01:75:28,360
and you don't like each other.

390
01:75:29,800 --> 01:75:32,200
All right, so that would be a possibility.

391
01:75:32,200 --> 01:75:34,720
Again, the idea there is that at the margin,

392
01:75:34,720 --> 01:75:37,520
if you're thinking, should I go and order this $10 dessert,

393
01:75:37,520 --> 01:75:39,480
if you know that you're paying the full check,

394
01:75:39,480 --> 01:75:42,600
you will order the dessert as long as you value that dessert

395
01:75:42,600 --> 01:75:46,440
at $10 dessert more than $10,

396
01:75:46,440 --> 01:75:49,440
because you know this will raise my check by $10.

397
01:75:49,440 --> 01:75:51,240
It'll also raise the checks of a bunch of other people

398
01:75:51,240 --> 01:75:52,440
that I don't care about by $10,

399
01:75:52,440 --> 01:75:54,400
but I don't care about them, so too bad.

400
01:75:54,400 --> 01:75:55,840
I guess if you truly dislike them,

401
01:75:55,840 --> 01:75:58,960
you might actually enjoy impoverishing them.

402
01:75:58,960 --> 01:76:01,640
Just say a bunch of people where you're like,

403
01:76:01,640 --> 01:76:03,960
I could take them or leave them, whatever.

404
01:76:03,960 --> 01:76:06,880
So why you're eating with these people is a fair question.

405
01:76:06,880 --> 01:76:10,200
All right, so anyway, this Landsberg thought experiment

406
01:76:10,200 --> 01:76:12,720
is the same idea behind selling the whole company

407
01:76:12,720 --> 01:76:16,200
to the CEO so that you turn every company

408
01:76:16,200 --> 01:76:17,400
into sole proprietorship.

409
01:76:18,280 --> 01:76:22,000
Now, another solutionable hazard,

410
01:76:22,000 --> 01:76:24,200
and this is one that is 100 times more realistic

411
01:76:24,200 --> 01:76:26,760
than Landsberg's one, maybe even a million times

412
01:76:26,760 --> 01:76:28,560
more realistic than Landsberg's one.

413
01:76:29,840 --> 01:76:31,360
It's one, as I think I mentioned already,

414
01:76:31,360 --> 01:76:32,760
Robin Hanson in his dissertation,

415
01:76:32,760 --> 01:76:34,680
he had a whole chapter on this idea.

416
01:76:34,680 --> 01:76:37,240
It's one of these ideas that is common sense and it's great

417
01:76:37,240 --> 01:76:39,320
and still hardly anybody does anything with it.

418
01:76:39,320 --> 01:76:43,520
In a way, it's too obvious, but it's so doable

419
01:76:43,520 --> 01:76:46,600
that it's frustrating that it gets a little attention.

420
01:76:46,600 --> 01:76:50,120
So here is another remedy from lower hazard,

421
01:76:50,120 --> 01:76:53,640
and that is for a contractual punishment.

422
01:76:59,520 --> 01:77:01,360
So here you solve the Maranza problem,

423
01:77:01,360 --> 01:77:05,920
not in this weird way of having everyone pay the entire check.

424
01:77:05,920 --> 01:77:09,160
You solve it by saying, look, if I,

425
01:77:09,160 --> 01:77:12,760
the following things count as cheating on this deal.

426
01:77:12,760 --> 01:77:14,520
If I'm ever caught doing these things,

427
01:77:14,520 --> 01:77:16,520
I owe you a lot of money.

428
01:77:16,520 --> 01:77:19,400
I owe you more than the money that I took from you

429
01:77:19,400 --> 01:77:20,840
by cheating.

430
01:77:21,960 --> 01:77:23,680
So for example, you have a contract that says

431
01:77:23,680 --> 01:77:25,760
if the boss ever catches you playing Tetris,

432
01:77:25,760 --> 01:77:28,200
you owe the boss $5,000.

433
01:77:28,200 --> 01:77:30,000
And you sign that contract.

434
01:77:30,000 --> 01:77:31,560
Now you might say, I don't know, that contract

435
01:77:31,560 --> 01:77:32,960
seems pretty dangerous.

436
01:77:32,960 --> 01:77:35,440
Maybe he's just gonna go and falsely accuse me.

437
01:77:35,440 --> 01:77:36,800
All right, well, if that's your concern,

438
01:77:36,800 --> 01:77:39,000
then don't sign the contract.

439
01:77:39,000 --> 01:77:43,600
But you would say, fine, I'll go and agree to a $500 fine.

440
01:77:43,600 --> 01:77:46,880
So something that, like, any time that you arrange it

441
01:77:46,880 --> 01:77:50,280
so that it is, the fine is large enough

442
01:77:50,280 --> 01:77:52,200
that it's not worth it for you to do it.

443
01:77:52,200 --> 01:77:54,200
And then, of course, the employer doesn't need

444
01:77:54,200 --> 01:77:56,520
to put a lot of resources into monitoring you.

445
01:77:56,520 --> 01:77:59,360
They only need to do an occasional random audit.

446
01:77:59,360 --> 01:78:00,680
They can just have some dice.

447
01:78:00,680 --> 01:78:02,200
And if the dice all come up the wrong way,

448
01:78:02,200 --> 01:78:03,760
today's audit day.

449
01:78:03,760 --> 01:78:05,400
Of course, you don't tell people that.

450
01:78:05,400 --> 01:78:08,720
You just go and do a surprise inspection.

451
01:78:08,720 --> 01:78:11,240
And then you find out what's really going on.

452
01:78:11,240 --> 01:78:12,560
All right, so I think, remember, like,

453
01:78:12,560 --> 01:78:15,600
I think it was Tetris actually had a boss coming button

454
01:78:15,600 --> 01:78:16,920
where you click it and then it's,

455
01:78:16,920 --> 01:78:18,560
it hides the Tetris instantly.

456
01:78:18,560 --> 01:78:21,040
There isn't even, like, a minimized Tetris icon.

457
01:78:21,040 --> 01:78:22,960
It just completely disappears.

458
01:78:22,960 --> 01:78:24,920
So you could do that.

459
01:78:24,920 --> 01:78:27,480
But anyway, so the idea is if the less informed

460
01:78:27,480 --> 01:78:31,320
can pay to observe the more informed,

461
01:78:31,320 --> 01:78:34,360
then they can enforce good behavior at a low cost

462
01:78:34,360 --> 01:78:38,600
with random monitoring combined with severe punishment.

463
01:78:38,600 --> 01:78:39,720
Severe punishment.

464
01:78:39,720 --> 01:78:42,600
So it can't be heads I win, tails I break even.

465
01:78:42,600 --> 01:78:45,960
In that case, everybody's going to want to cheat.

466
01:78:45,960 --> 01:78:48,680
But on the other hand, if it's heads I win,

467
01:78:48,680 --> 01:78:53,520
and tails I go and I lose three times my gain from cheating,

468
01:78:53,520 --> 01:78:56,680
then the expected gain from cheating is negative.

469
01:78:56,680 --> 01:78:58,760
And in which case, the boss may need to put in

470
01:78:58,760 --> 01:79:01,200
very few resources in actually monitoring you.

471
01:79:02,640 --> 01:79:04,880
Right, and that is the idea.

472
01:79:04,880 --> 01:79:07,960
Now, there are many examples of how this can be done.

473
01:79:07,960 --> 01:79:09,720
You may say, well, isn't it already done?

474
01:79:09,720 --> 01:79:11,680
Well, so to some extent,

475
01:79:11,680 --> 01:79:13,160
but as I think I already mentioned,

476
01:79:13,160 --> 01:79:16,200
in all countries where the law descends

477
01:79:16,200 --> 01:79:17,880
from that of England,

478
01:79:17,880 --> 01:79:20,720
there is a rule saying that you cannot agree

479
01:79:20,720 --> 01:79:22,600
to a punitive punishment.

480
01:79:22,600 --> 01:79:26,480
So you can contractually agree to compensate someone

481
01:79:26,480 --> 01:79:28,200
for what you have cost them,

482
01:79:28,200 --> 01:79:30,560
but you can't contractually agree to more.

483
01:79:31,760 --> 01:79:33,560
Right, and then Robin points out,

484
01:79:33,560 --> 01:79:35,440
well, that's highly inefficient.

485
01:79:35,440 --> 01:79:38,640
Essentially, it's saying, you can sign a contract saying,

486
01:79:38,640 --> 01:79:40,360
heads I win, tails I break even.

487
01:79:41,440 --> 01:79:42,880
You can't sign a contract saying,

488
01:79:42,880 --> 01:79:45,280
heads I win and tails I lose.

489
01:79:45,280 --> 01:79:47,680
And of course, to convince people not to take a risk,

490
01:79:47,680 --> 01:79:49,560
you really need to put in that data,

491
01:79:49,560 --> 01:79:51,760
that actual benefit that you're getting.

492
01:79:51,760 --> 01:79:56,600
That actual bad outcome, or else it is a good gamble.

493
01:79:56,600 --> 01:79:57,440
Yes.

494
01:79:57,440 --> 01:79:58,280
Isn't this what a bond is?

495
01:79:58,280 --> 01:80:23,280
You put it up the money beforehand.


1
01:80:00,000 --> 01:80:02,700
Yes, I bet you, so again, they're probably,

2
01:80:02,700 --> 01:80:05,160
like in English, in English law systems,

3
01:80:05,640 --> 01:80:07,540
the person who forfeited the bond might actually be able

4
01:80:07,540 --> 01:80:09,720
to go and sue and say, you know, this contract,

5
01:80:09,720 --> 01:80:11,620
the way I signed it and knew everything that was going on,

6
01:80:11,980 --> 01:80:14,860
it's punitive because the value of the bond is more

7
01:80:14,860 --> 01:80:16,060
than the loss that I caused him.

8
01:80:16,900 --> 01:80:19,980
And if the person on the other side were to say, yes,

9
01:80:19,980 --> 01:80:21,780
but my odds of catching him are only one in three,

10
01:80:21,780 --> 01:80:24,440
so it needs to be punitive, you just lost.

11
01:80:25,840 --> 01:80:28,020
Right now, of course, if you've got a good lawyer,

12
01:80:28,020 --> 01:80:30,420
then you could just go and say, oh, no,

13
01:80:30,420 --> 01:80:32,140
it's not punitive at all.

14
01:80:32,540 --> 01:80:34,040
It's just that when someone plays Tetris,

15
01:80:34,040 --> 01:80:35,840
it costs my firm $5,000.

16
01:80:37,160 --> 01:80:38,140
And how is that?

17
01:80:38,460 --> 01:80:40,640
Well, yeah, when you really understand the way

18
01:80:40,640 --> 01:80:43,840
that a candy factory operates, you will realize

19
01:80:44,080 --> 01:80:47,360
that the playing of Tetris can endanger the candy,

20
01:80:47,360 --> 01:80:49,500
like a whole batch is going to be ruined

21
01:80:49,800 --> 01:80:52,740
if the person just looks down from their desk for one second.

22
01:80:53,240 --> 01:80:55,000
The candy could be destroyed, millions of dollars

23
01:80:55,000 --> 01:80:56,360
worth of candy, really, it should be more.

24
01:80:56,360 --> 01:81:00,320
And if you convince a judge that this is true, then you won.

25
01:81:00,760 --> 01:81:04,100
And then you did impose your punitive punishment by stealth.

26
01:81:04,920 --> 01:81:11,560
But there are many uses of contractual punishments

27
01:81:11,560 --> 01:81:12,720
that are currently not done.

28
01:81:13,060 --> 01:81:15,480
Things like trying to get an opportunity to get a job.

29
01:81:16,500 --> 01:81:19,540
So right now, you could offer to go and work for someone for free.

30
01:81:20,200 --> 01:81:22,480
But if you said, look, I'll go and work for you,

31
01:81:22,480 --> 01:81:25,800
pay me a normal salary, but during this trial period,

