64
01:82:44,940 --> 01:82:47,900
Right? And if you're willing to sign a contract like this,

65
01:82:47,900 --> 01:82:49,940
this is a way that you could get a better rate

66
01:82:49,940 --> 01:82:50,760
than you currently get.

67
01:82:51,380 --> 01:82:53,040
There are, you know, so, again, there's all kinds of things

68
01:82:53,040 --> 01:82:54,160
that you can do to endanger your life.

69
01:82:54,660 --> 01:82:57,480
Right now, for like health or life insurance, if you were

70
01:82:57,480 --> 01:82:59,420
to say, look, if I am ever caught doing any

71
01:82:59,420 --> 01:83:02,560
of the following things, not only do you not have to pay me,

72
01:83:02,900 --> 01:83:04,200
but my estate owes you money.

73
01:83:06,140 --> 01:83:08,860
And, of course, contractually you'd say, and you are,

74
01:83:08,860 --> 01:83:12,020
and in my, this supersedes any other will that I make,

75
01:83:12,320 --> 01:83:14,580
and the insurance company must be compensated first

76
01:83:14,940 --> 01:83:16,280
for my dishonesty.

77
01:83:18,000 --> 01:83:19,660
Right? And this would be a way, of course, to get,

78
01:83:19,660 --> 01:83:20,940
and why would anyone sign the contract?

79
01:83:20,940 --> 01:83:23,500
To get lower rates, to get better coverage.

80
01:83:24,600 --> 01:83:27,780
Right? And this way you could get around a wide range

81
01:83:27,780 --> 01:83:29,820
of moral hazard problems that right now we just feel

82
01:83:29,820 --> 01:83:30,500
like we have to live with.

83
01:83:32,080 --> 01:83:33,860
Now, another interesting thing about moral hazards.

84
01:83:33,860 --> 01:83:37,000
So, you possibly, I guess you're about old enough

85
01:83:37,000 --> 01:83:38,480
to remember the Obamacare debate.

86
01:83:39,520 --> 01:83:40,900
Now, during the Obamacare debate,

87
01:83:41,260 --> 01:83:45,420
you might remember a bunch of supportive economists saying,

88
01:83:45,420 --> 01:83:47,640
look, you can't really have a free market insurance

89
01:83:47,640 --> 01:83:50,040
because of these fundamental problems of moral hazard

90
01:83:50,040 --> 01:83:50,940
and adverse selection.

91
01:83:51,420 --> 01:83:52,820
We'll get to the adverse selection problem in a minute.

92
01:83:53,380 --> 01:83:54,900
But now, just think about moral hazard.

93
01:83:55,420 --> 01:83:57,560
Right? So, what in the world does any system

94
01:83:57,560 --> 01:83:59,800
of government funded medicine do about moral hazard?

95
01:84:01,660 --> 01:84:02,000
What's that?

96
01:84:02,280 --> 01:84:03,620
Yes, exacerbate it.

97
01:84:03,920 --> 01:84:04,980
Yes, it doesn't look like you.

98
01:84:05,120 --> 01:84:07,600
In other words, they just, like the normal thing is

99
01:84:07,600 --> 01:84:10,740
to say everybody gets total coverage, and yes,

100
01:84:10,740 --> 01:84:12,280
this leads to a lot of extra risk taking.

101
01:84:12,280 --> 01:84:13,300
What are we going to do about it?

102
01:84:13,600 --> 01:84:15,680
Nothing. We're just going to let people do,

103
01:84:15,680 --> 01:84:17,700
have totally unhealthy lifestyles

104
01:84:17,700 --> 01:84:20,560
and do whatever they want and get full coverage.

105
01:84:20,560 --> 01:84:23,300
Now, once in a while, there are very specific measures

106
01:84:23,740 --> 01:84:27,880
that people say are due to the existence of government funding,

107
01:84:28,140 --> 01:84:29,760
things like motorcycle helmet laws.

108
01:84:30,560 --> 01:84:32,260
We say, well, since the government would have to pay

109
01:84:32,260 --> 01:84:33,480
for you if you got in an accident,

110
01:84:33,480 --> 01:84:35,880
you have to wear a helmet, or things

111
01:84:35,880 --> 01:84:37,300
like anti-smoking programs.

112
01:84:37,300 --> 01:84:39,080
So, since government has to pay for you,

113
01:84:39,400 --> 01:84:41,380
if you get lung cancer, then we're going to go

114
01:84:41,380 --> 01:84:42,240
and discourage smoking.

115
01:84:42,760 --> 01:84:45,880
There's some famous work by Kip Viscusi and others showing,

116
01:84:45,880 --> 01:84:48,080
as far as I've heard, no informed person actually

117
01:84:48,080 --> 01:84:50,940
disputes it, that smoking actually saves taxpayers money.

118
01:84:52,040 --> 01:84:53,440
Because you're a lot more likely

119
01:84:53,440 --> 01:84:55,880
to die before you even collect Medicare.

120
01:84:56,960 --> 01:84:57,180
All right?

121
01:84:57,180 --> 01:84:59,660
So, actually, if anything, by this reasoning,

122
01:84:59,980 --> 01:85:03,600
the government should be subsidizing smoking.

123
01:85:03,800 --> 01:85:05,880
Like, again, basically the kinds of unhelpful behavior

124
01:85:05,880 --> 01:85:07,160
that kill you quickly and young.

125
01:85:07,860 --> 01:85:11,360
Right? And, again, that's purely in terms of Morazzo, but.

126
01:85:11,780 --> 01:85:15,820
Yes. So, what's striking

127
01:85:15,820 --> 01:85:19,160
to me is that economists were supportive of Obamacare.

128
01:85:19,160 --> 01:85:21,620
We're very quick just to go and repeat a bunch of slogans

129
01:85:21,620 --> 01:85:24,820
out of the textbook, rather than going through them one by one

130
01:85:24,820 --> 01:85:27,760
and seeing, wait a second, does this slogan actually have

131
01:85:27,760 --> 01:85:29,700
anything to do with the program we're talking about?

132
01:85:30,140 --> 01:85:32,180
Or might there even be a possible tension between it?

133
01:85:33,060 --> 01:85:35,460
Right? And, again, so you can certainly imagine,

134
01:85:35,760 --> 01:85:37,860
like a fully socialized medicine program

135
01:85:37,860 --> 01:85:39,560
that would do things like this.

136
01:85:40,260 --> 01:85:42,020
So, you could say, fine, we cover everybody,

137
01:85:42,020 --> 01:85:45,060
but every year there's an annual way, and we go,

138
01:85:45,060 --> 01:85:46,980
and you have to pay extra taxes if you're too heavy.

139
01:85:47,580 --> 01:85:50,400
And then, like, we go and we monitor your physical activity,

140
01:85:50,400 --> 01:85:52,840
and you have to pay extra taxes if you're physically inactive.

141
01:85:52,840 --> 01:85:55,340
And you could imagine a whole bunch of different fines

142
01:85:55,340 --> 01:85:58,600
that you would have to pay based upon your behavior.

143
01:85:59,640 --> 01:86:01,640
I don't know of any country, even Singapore,

144
01:86:01,760 --> 01:86:04,080
that actually goes and imposes things like this.

145
01:86:04,720 --> 01:86:06,520
But, and, again, like, why not?

146
01:86:06,960 --> 01:86:08,460
Well, it's certainly not because they're aware

147
01:86:08,460 --> 01:86:10,680
of the Morazzo problem, and this is some clever solution.

148
01:86:11,020 --> 01:86:13,660
It's more like either they are unaware

149
01:86:13,660 --> 01:86:15,760
of the Morazzo problem, or they just said, you know,

150
01:86:15,760 --> 01:86:16,680
we're going to do about it.

151
01:86:16,680 --> 01:86:18,720
Like, we're just going to live with really bad incentives.

152
01:86:19,360 --> 01:86:22,320
Because we think that no one should ever have to worry

153
01:86:22,320 --> 01:86:23,520
about their health insurance coverage.

154
01:86:24,420 --> 01:86:26,200
Right? Now, there's also something odd about this

155
01:86:26,200 --> 01:86:28,580
when you think about the populist idea.

156
01:86:28,580 --> 01:86:31,460
Look, everyone should have the best possible coverage

157
01:86:31,460 --> 01:86:33,240
because health is the most precious of all things.

158
01:86:34,180 --> 01:86:36,080
Well, if it's the most precious of all things,

159
01:86:36,120 --> 01:86:37,740
then how do you explain all these people

160
01:86:37,740 --> 01:86:38,940
who take all these dumb risks?

161
01:86:40,140 --> 01:86:42,180
How do you explain all the unhealthy lifestyles?

162
01:86:42,180 --> 01:86:45,260
Well, they don't know that it's the most important

163
01:86:45,260 --> 01:86:45,960
of all things.

164
01:86:46,480 --> 01:86:48,280
Right? Well, then why don't we go and give them incentives

165
01:86:48,280 --> 01:86:51,980
to go and teach them to do the things

166
01:86:51,980 --> 01:86:53,060
that are actually best for them?

167
01:86:53,300 --> 01:86:53,680
No, no, no.

168
01:86:53,680 --> 01:86:55,760
That would go and be a horrible abridgment

169
01:86:55,760 --> 01:86:56,640
of their freedom, right?

170
01:86:56,640 --> 01:87:00,160
So it's one thing, like, it's terrible to go and not pay

171
01:87:00,160 --> 01:87:02,240
for everything, but also terrible if you go

172
01:87:02,240 --> 01:87:04,960
and impose any rules upon the recipients.

173
01:87:05,820 --> 01:87:07,920
Right? Again, well, how about you could have free coverage

174
01:87:07,920 --> 01:87:10,120
as long as you agree to abide by all of our rules?

175
01:87:10,120 --> 01:87:12,920
No, no, that's dehumanizing, too, so can't have that.

176
01:87:14,280 --> 01:87:14,520
All right.

177
01:87:14,720 --> 01:87:16,620
So, anyway, bottom line, contractual punishment,

178
01:87:16,800 --> 01:87:20,120
great idea, very underused, strangely illegal,

179
01:87:20,220 --> 01:87:21,780
barely studied, right?

180
01:87:21,780 --> 01:87:24,880
So it seems like it deserves way more attention,

181
01:87:24,880 --> 01:87:26,500
and especially if you could do something empirical with it,

182
01:87:26,500 --> 01:87:27,900
I think it would be awesome, right?

183
01:87:27,900 --> 01:87:28,980
And Robin Hanson is right here.

184
01:87:28,980 --> 01:87:30,000
I'm sure he'd love to talk to you about it.

185
01:87:31,120 --> 01:87:31,520
All right.

186
01:87:32,400 --> 01:87:34,940
So now we come to next topic.

187
01:87:35,380 --> 01:87:38,340
So this was the economic argument for Obamacare,

188
01:87:38,340 --> 01:87:40,580
which actually is relevant to Obamacare.

189
01:87:41,360 --> 01:87:41,640
All right.

190
01:87:41,640 --> 01:87:44,340
So, I mean, like, so I got a post-pricing, I'll say, like,

191
01:87:44,640 --> 01:87:46,500
adverse hazard, moral selection, whatever.

192
01:87:47,040 --> 01:87:49,280
It's like, don't even care about exactly what the term is.

193
01:87:49,280 --> 01:87:51,840
We're sort of throwing out a word salad of arguments.

194
01:87:52,440 --> 01:87:52,620
All right.

195
01:87:52,620 --> 01:87:53,440
But, anyway, adverse selection.

196
01:87:53,960 --> 01:87:56,960
So this is a much more complex form of asymmetric information,

197
01:87:57,540 --> 01:87:59,280
and the basic idea of this is you know your

198
01:87:59,280 --> 01:88:02,400
own characteristics, but other people treat you based upon the

199
01:88:02,400 --> 01:88:03,960
average characteristics of people

200
01:88:03,960 --> 01:88:05,680
who look like you superficially.

201
01:88:05,680 --> 01:88:07,280
People who are superficially like you.

202
01:88:08,200 --> 01:88:10,900
What this means is that if you happen to be above average

203
01:88:10,900 --> 01:88:13,280
for people who look like you on the surface,

204
01:88:13,920 --> 01:88:16,600
then you are more likely to decide there's no point being

205
01:88:16,600 --> 01:88:19,840
in the market, and when the people who are better

206
01:88:19,840 --> 01:88:22,000
than average leave, what does this do to the average?

207
01:88:23,440 --> 01:88:26,240
It lowers the average, and then this means

208
01:88:26,240 --> 01:88:28,440
that there's another, there's a new person who's now the best

209
01:88:28,440 --> 01:88:30,600
person who's way above average, and he may want to leave,

210
01:88:30,600 --> 01:88:32,480
and then the next person may want to leave,

211
01:88:32,480 --> 01:88:35,980
and as a result you may even theoretically, it is possible,

212
01:88:36,460 --> 01:88:40,760
not certain, but possible, to get this full unraveling result

213
01:88:40,760 --> 01:88:42,440
kind of like with the centipede game,

214
01:88:43,560 --> 01:88:45,640
where the entire market disappears.

215
01:88:47,640 --> 01:88:47,940
All right.

216
01:88:47,940 --> 01:88:50,440
So then imagine a system where I say, look, I go

217
01:88:50,440 --> 01:88:52,600
and I fail the bottom half of every other class.

218
01:88:53,400 --> 01:88:54,920
Right. Well, then anyone who knows they're going

219
01:88:54,920 --> 01:88:56,440
to be the bottom half of the class drops, right.

220
01:88:56,440 --> 01:88:57,440
What's the point of being in class

221
01:88:57,440 --> 01:88:58,280
if you know you're going to fail?

222
01:88:58,880 --> 01:89:01,480
But if then I say, well, gee, now there's a new person

223
01:89:01,480 --> 01:89:04,120
in the class, and I fail that bottom half, right,

224
01:89:04,120 --> 01:89:06,960
and then that bottom half drops out, and then the bottom half

225
01:89:06,960 --> 01:89:09,160
of the remaining ones, and then eventually we're left

226
01:89:09,160 --> 01:89:11,320
with just one person, and then I don't know what the rule is.

227
01:89:11,320 --> 01:89:12,320
I guess 50-50.

228
01:89:12,320 --> 01:89:16,560
So that would be a case of unraveling if I'm down to either

229
01:89:16,560 --> 01:89:19,440
one or zero students, depending upon how you define the rule.

230
01:89:19,440 --> 01:89:24,440
Okay. So that is the idea of adverse selection.

231
01:89:24,440 --> 01:89:28,240
Right. And of course this was indeed a key argument

232
01:89:28,240 --> 01:89:32,000
for Obamacare, and it's one that has been noted in many contexts.

233
01:89:32,000 --> 01:89:34,120
So you've heard of the adverse selection death spiral.

234
01:89:34,120 --> 01:89:34,640
All right.

235
01:89:34,640 --> 01:89:36,880
Well, this is what we're talking about.

236
01:89:36,880 --> 01:89:39,560
The death spiral is the extreme case, but it's one

237
01:89:39,560 --> 01:89:42,520
where if everybody can go and get insurance

238
01:89:42,520 --> 01:89:44,560
at the same price regardless of their health conditions,

239
01:89:44,560 --> 01:89:46,800
then the first people who want to go

240
01:89:46,800 --> 01:89:48,400
and buy insurance are the sickest people.

241
01:89:48,400 --> 01:89:51,560
And as a result, the price of insurance is high.

242
01:89:51,560 --> 01:89:52,880
And then who doesn't want insurance?

243
01:89:52,880 --> 01:89:53,480
Healthy people.

244
01:89:53,480 --> 01:89:54,680
Who doesn't want insurance?

245
01:89:54,680 --> 01:89:55,280
Healthy people.

246
01:89:55,280 --> 01:89:58,280
And then who doesn't want insurance?

247
01:89:58,280 --> 01:89:59,280
Healthy people.

248
01:89:59,280 --> 01:90:01,280
Because I don't want to pay $10,000 a year

249
01:90:01,280 --> 01:90:02,880
when I've got no problems, especially if I know

250
01:90:02,880 --> 01:90:05,480
that if I ever get really sick, I can go and buy insurance

251
01:90:05,480 --> 01:90:07,920
for the same price that it's currently at.

252
01:90:07,920 --> 01:90:08,120
All right.

253
01:90:08,120 --> 01:90:09,840
And we will pick up with this next class.

254
01:90:09,840 --> 01:90:11,120
Small surcharge.

255
01:90:11,120 --> 01:90:13,000
Yes. With a small surcharge.

256
01:90:13,000 --> 01:90:16,600
Yes. That is a first step to going

257
01:90:16,600 --> 01:90:18,040
and reducing adverse selection,

258
01:90:18,040 --> 01:90:21,680
but of course it depends upon how severe it is.

259
01:90:21,680 --> 01:90:28,680
Okay.


